The Ministry of Justice organizes a meeting on "Dissemination of the Royal Decree on Mitigation and Amnesty" and strengthens the work of requesting amnesty
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Justice has organized a meeting on "Dissemination of the Royal Decree on Mitigation and Amnesty" and strengthen the work for amnesty under the chairmanship of Mr. Keut Rith, Minister of Justice and Chairman of the National Commission for Review and Evaluation of the List. The convict asked for a commutation of the sentence and a pardon.
The meeting will take place on February 17, 2021 at the Ministry of Justice with the participation of the National Commission, the Ministry of Interior and the Capital and Provincial Committees.
He added that in 2020, the King issued a royal decree pardoning and commuting the sentences of a total of 931 prisoners, 90 of whom were women. Of these, 55 were pardoned and 876 were pardoned.
Article 7 of the Royal Decree stipulates that a convict can receive a commutation of his sentence only if he has served one-third of the sentence imposed by the court. For life imprisonment, the convict must first be sentenced to 10 years.
In the same article, it is stated that the period of commutation of sentence shall be determined as follows:
Article 9 of the Royal Decree also states that prisoners can receive a one-time commutation of their sentences within 12 months. A convict may receive a commutation of multiple sentences only if he or she has served half of the sentence imposed by the court, but subject to the conditions set out in the first paragraph above.
Inmates sentenced to life in prison after being reduced to 30 years could receive multiple commutations. However, even though the prisoner has been pardoned several times, the total sentence must be at least 20 years.
