The case of Ly Usaphea, former director of the Stung Treng Provincial Land Management Department, who issued a title deed to 528 hectares of state forest land, is now being investigated by a judge after prosecutors charged him and asked for his detention.
(Stung Treng): Mr. Ly Usaphea, Director of the Land Management Department of Ratanakkiri Province, is under investigation and supervision by the Stung Treng Provincial Court after the prosecutor decided to charge him and request the detainee to issue an illegal title deed on a dense forest area. More than 500 hectares to one person when he was the director of the Department of Land Management Stung Treng.
On the morning of February 16, 2021, the investigating judge led the police to the location of the forest land where Mr. Ly Usaphea issued an irregular title deed to check the situation.
In Closing Order No. 227 dated 13 August 2020 of Mr. Chet Khemara, Prosecutor of Stung Treng Provincial Prosecutor's Office concluded that Ly Usaphea had issued a title deed to a dense forest land in a protected area. Mixed without the people's enjoyable land, and there is no transfer of ownership from one to another, only the title is issued to one person.
The prosecutor decided to charge Mr. Ly Usaphea with forgery of public documents under Article 629 of the Penal Code, so the prosecutor should detain the accused Ly Usaphea to ensure that the accused is kept in court for legal proceedings. .
Currently, the investigating judge of Stung Treng Provincial Court is working on the above case and decided not to detain him, but named Ly Usaphea under the court's supervision. Separately, Ly Usaphea has been working as the director of the Department of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Cadastral Affairs in Ratanakkiri Province as usual.
