(Kratie): Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, stated that the development of plantations of Sing Bay Otec (Cambodia) has value in supplying wood raw materials for the wood processing industry and it is An agro-industrial process in Cambodia's first plantation plantation This plantation will play an important role in reducing pressure on natural timber.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra made this remark during an interview with national and international journalists on the occasion of leading more than 300 young people to a two-day camp on the 14th to 15th. February 2021 at the Customs Headquarters in Prey Lang Wildlife Sanctuary, Kratie Province and visit the plantation of Sing Bay Otec (Cambodia).
On that occasion, the spokesman also expressed support and said that investment in plantations is important because it will reduce the pressure on the use of natural wood and the establishment of the wood processing industry. It will also help create jobs and increase incomes for people in the area to participate in national economic growth.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra: "Because of the current global trend, they are starting to turn to the use of processed wood, plantation, and they do not give the price of wood that uses natural wood, our country is also beginning to see the trend. "Sometimes hotels, guesthouses with beautiful wood do not have many visitors because they understand the importance of natural resources."
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the demand for furniture that people still need it is a pressure on natural resources and deforestation, but if people refuse to use furniture made of rosewood, but turn to furniture. Made from plantations, it helps to reduce the pressure on natural resources.
The young spokesman emphasized: “The plantation of Sing Bay Otec (Cambodia) is an agro-industrial process in the plantations in Cambodia for the first time. "It plays an important role in reducing the pressure on natural wood, because our country needs more than 2 million cubic meters of wood per year, so this is where the pressure on natural wood."
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that at the same time, the Royal Government has also encouraged agro-industry, plantations, plantations to provide raw materials to reduce pressure on natural wood, and if we do not have the need to use natural wood is nothing but No pressure on natural wood, so this is a task that we are working hard for the Royal Government to cooperate with neighboring countries to prevent illegal timber trade and so on.
