Khatibzadeh made the remarks after being questioned by Iran's intelligence minister last week, saying that continued pressure from the West could push Tehran to turn. Nuclear weapons.
In May 2018, former US President Donald Trump withdrew the United States from the JCPOA, or the 2015 Iran Nuclear Agreement, and re-imposed sanctions on Iran. The nuclear deal recognizes Iran's right to peaceful use of nuclear energy, but Iran has promised not to develop nuclear weapons in exchange for US and European sanctions on Iran's oil exports.
In November 2020, the Iranian parliament passed a law requiring the Iranian government to suspend inspections by the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) at its nuclear power plants and increase uranium enrichment. Under the JCPOA agreement, if sanctions remain unresolved or relaxed.
The new US government, led by President Joe Biden, has demanded that Iran suspend uranium enrichment and return to full compliance with the JCPOA before Washington agrees to discuss lifting sanctions.
