Letters from readers: How did the coup in Myanmar affect the Cambodian economy?
(Myanmar): A coup d'etat organized by military leaders took place in Myanmar on February 1, 2021. Aung San Suu Kyi, a state adviser and leader of the National League for Democracy (NLD), along with several key party leaders, has been arrested and Myanmar has been declared a state of emergency. .
It should be noted that Aung San Suu Kyi's party won a landslide victory in November 2020. However, there have been complaints about the election results being rigged, despite allegations by the Myanmar Election Commission. The protests were backed by the military, and the coup comes just before the start of a new parliamentary and parliamentary term in Myanmar.
The international community and Western powers such as the United Nations, the European Union and the United Kingdom have condemned the coup. US President Joe Biden has announced sanctions against Myanmar's coup leader.
For the countries in ASEAN, generally take a position not to interfere in the internal affairs of member countries. ASEAN member states see this as an internal matter of Myanmar and have only called for talks between the parties to the conflict to return the situation to normal soon.
Coup d'état could cause real political instability In recent days, there have been protests by pro-Aung San Suu Kyi supporters, which could hurt the economic situation in Myanmar. The United States has also announced sanctions on Myanmar. All of these factors will have a devastating effect on the economy of a country that has only just begun to see the light of day for growth over the past decade.
As we all know, Myanmar is a country rich in natural resources, so the negative impact on normal economic activities and the superpowers' sanctions could force Myanmar to do business. On its natural resources more than ever.
These can have a long-term social and economic impact on Myanmar. How can this coup affect our Cambodian economy? In general, coups in Myanmar will have little direct impact on the Cambodian economy. Trade relations between the two countries are still small.
In 2019, exports from Cambodia to Myanmar were only about 4 million US dollars. In fact, Myanmar is an important exporter of garments and garments and is also one of Cambodia's main competitors in this field.
Coups and political instability in Myanmar could hurt the country's exports, which in turn could have some positive effects on Cambodia's garment sector. On the other hand, if such a political situation in Myanmar persists, foreign investors may withdraw their capital from this country to invest in other countries in the region, including Cambodia.
