Understand the law: tenure
(Phnom Penh): In general, each person has the right to an object according to the situation of different legal relations, among which we see that the right of possession is one of the most comprehensive of the rights over Other stuff.
Tenure rights are exercised in the management of both movable and immovable property in all forms, such as through contracts (leases, lease agreements, etc.) and non-contractual relationships (possession of selected objects or objects). Which has no owner, for example).
To better understand what tenure is and what it looks like? For this week, Fresh News' Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, would like to present the following topics on "Occupancy" to the following students:
Tenure is a type of foreclosure that is more distinctive than other types of foreclosure because it can be created, whether for a legitimate reason or not, that is, only the possession of the object. Directly or indirectly, it is sufficient for the establishment of tenure.
Please note that direct tenure is an occupation that occupies the object directly through legal reasons established with the indirect occupant. Indirect possession, on the other hand, is the possession of objects through others after establishing a legal relationship.
In each possession, there is always a transfer of tenure in it as well, with the transfer of possession being the act of changing the status of the occupant through consent, succession, succession, or through other reasons determined by Law.
The occupancy, on the other hand, ceases when the occupant loses his grip on the object. And for the termination of the occupation is also divided into two (two) the same, with the termination of direct occupation and the termination of indirect occupation.
