General Knowledge: Aung San Suu Kyi, the world's longest-serving political prisoner, understands the political life of this outstanding woman
Aung San Suu Kyi is a Myanmar politician and leader of Myanmar's main opposition party, the National League for Democracy (NLD). Before becoming an international celebrity, she was held under house arrest for more than 21 years until she was named the world's longest-serving political prisoner from the 20th. July 1989 to November 13, 2010.
After the coup, which was strongly condemned by the international community, the military declared a one-year state of emergency and handed over all power to General Min Ong Leang, who promised to hold new general elections. Free and fair after the end of the state of emergency, however, the exact date of the election has not been confirmed.
On May 2, 2008, after Cyclone Nargis hit Myanmar, her dilapidated house was damaged, the roof was cut off and the electricity was cut off, so she had to use candles for lighting at night. She does not have a generator. But in August 2009, it was reported that her home had been renovated.
Aung San Suu Kyi became involved in politics in search of democracy for Myanmar, and on September 27, 1988, she helped form the National League for Democracy (NLD). She has been under house arrest several times since 1989, and was released on bail in 2010.
During her arrest, she may have had the option to remain out of jail unless she left the country, but she declined the offer. All the while under house arrest, she sacrificed her life with Buddhist prayers and study of the Buddha's teachings.
1996: Attack on November 9, 1996. Aung San Suu Kyi's convoy is attacked, with a group of about 200 people using chains, iron rods and stones to attack her convoy. The incident is believed to have been carried out by the United Solidarity and Development Association (USDA), which hired the attackers.
UN Intervention During her house arrest, the United Nations (UN) focused on intervening in the release of Aung San Suu Kyi. The United Nations intends to mediate negotiations between the militant leader and her.
As a result, on May 6, 2002, under secret talks led by the UN, the government decided to release her. However, she was repeatedly detained for various reasons.
Meanwhile, British Prime Minister Gordon Brown has warned the Myanmar government of diplomatic and economic pressure. During that time, Aung San Suu Kyi had the opportunity to meet with state leaders and hold talks with key officials, but was closely monitored by the government. Finally, on the afternoon of November 13, 2010, the political prisoner was released.
On April 1, 2012, Aung San Suu Kyi's NLD party won 43 of the 45 parliamentary elections and officially declared her leader. Opposition parties in Myanmar's lower house. On May 2, 2012, NLD lawmakers, including her own, were sworn in.
The world is now focusing on the Southeast Asian country, which is slowly making its way to democracy, as the election results show that Aung San Suu Kyi has garnered a lot of support. Many analysts have questioned whether she could reach the presidency of Myanmar if the country's military power remains strong.
