Kork Chak Temple, more than 1,000 years old, was repaired by APSARA Authority
(Siem Reap): Two towers of Kork Chak temple facing east, facing high damage due to the destruction of many small and large plants growing on the temple towers, causing tree roots to penetrate deep into the brick structure, causing The wall was split in two and repaired by experts from the APSARA Authority to prevent it from collapsing.
According to Ms. Chhun Ratana, archaeologist and director of the Kork Chak Temple Preservation Project of the Department of Temple Conservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, the damage to the two towers of Kork Chak Temple was caused by age-related vegetative factors. Human construction and climate.
She added that in 2015, the team repaired the dilapidated south tower at the front and south walls. In 2020, the team also completed the renovation of the North Tower, which collapsed in the southwest corner. Repairs to the Kork Chak temple are ongoing, with the team repairing the north wall and the northwest wall of the north tower to prevent it from collapsing and collapsing.
In the work of preserving the brick temple, which is more than a thousand years old, Ms. Ratana and her team emphasized the technique of repairing and reinforcing the structure of the brick temple by removing rotten and unsafe bricks and replacing them with some new bricks. If necessary to strengthen the strength of the temple. Then remove the weeds, clean the small and large plants that grow on the temple and blow the soil out of the bricks.
Not only that, the team also pays attention to closing small and large holes that allow water or water to penetrate into the temple structure, easily affecting the temple to decompose. All of these repairs are in accordance with the restoration standards in the Angkor area, the use of traditional products and techniques, and in accordance with the original form of the temple.
