Cement Industry Association: Cambodian Cement Production Increases 7% Despite Kovid Crisis 19
(Phnom Penh): The Cement Industry Association of Cambodia (CCMA) on the evening of February 08, 2021 held a meeting to summarize the results of its production in the second half of 2020 and review its production status at the beginning of the year. This 2021, while Cambodia as well as the world are facing Kovid 19 disease.
The meeting was held at Hayyat Regency Hotel Phnom Penh under the chairmanship of Duke Leang Meng, Chairman of the Board of the Cambodian Cement Industry Association, and was also attended by representatives of five cement companies that are members of the Association in Cambodia and the Secretary General of the Association. Also.
Put Chantarith, secretary-general of the Cambodian Cement Industry Association (CCMA), confirmed to Fresh News that despite the Kovid 19 situation, Cambodia's cement production has not declined. Cement manufacturers, which are members of the association as a whole, increased by about 7 percent in the second half of 2020 and will continue to grow in early 2021.
Interior Ministry Spokesman: 44 people arrested for criminal and drug offenses
(Phnom Penh): At least 44 suspects were arrested in criminal and drug offenses across Cambodia on February 08, 2021 yesterday. This was confirmed by General Khieu Sopheak, spokesman for the Ministry of Interior, to Fresh News this morning, February 9.
General Khieu Sopheak added that of the 44 suspects, 31 were arrested in 16 criminal cases and 13 were arrested in six drug cases.
1. A 19-year-old Cambodian woman residing in Sangkat Choam Chao, Khan Por Senchey, Phnom Penh, traveling from the United States to continue her flight from South Korea to Cambodia on February 7, 2021. The test results of this woman provided by the National Institute of Public Health of the Ministry of Health are positive for Kovid-19 virus and the patient is currently being treated at Chak Angre Health Center in Phnom Penh.
2. A 26-year-old Chinese woman was staying at a hotel in Phnom Penh as a passenger from China. She continued her flight from South Korea to Cambodia on February 7, 2021. The test results of the woman, provided by the National Institutes of Public Health of the Ministry of Health, were positive for Kovid-19 virus, and the patient is currently being treated at the National Center for Tuberculosis and Hepatitis C in Phnom Penh.
Please note that the total number of air passengers with the above patients is 92, of which the above 2 individuals as well. The 90 passengers who tested positive for the Kovid-19 virus were screened for 14 days at one hotel in Phnom Penh, 46 at another hotel and 40 at the US Embassy.
