Myanmar's military chief delivers first national speech since coup, vows to hold new, free and fair elections
RANGON (Reuters) - Myanmar's military junta has called on the public to prioritize facts over sentiment, stressing that new elections will be held soon and power will be handed over to the winning party. This is according to the release by CNA on Monday night, February 8, 2021.
In his first speech since the coup a week ago, General Min Aung Hlaing said the military regime was different from previous military governments, stressing that appropriate ministers would be elected and that foreign policy Remains unchanged as countries will be encouraged to invest in Myanmar.
At the same time, he reiterated that there were irregularities in last year's election that were overlooked, and stressed that no organization can be above the law. However, he did not mention Aung San Suu Kyi.
In response, the Myanmar military has declared martial law in its major cities. The law will cover seven cities of Mandalay, banning people from protesting or gathering in groups of more than five.
