Minister of Environment: Cambodia acts as a strong supporter of multilateral action in response to climate change
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Say Sam Al, Chairman of the National Council for Sustainable Development and Minister of Environment, emphasized that Cambodia has acted as a country with strong support for multilateral activities in response to climate change.
Mr. Say Sam Al's remarks were made on the occasion of the launch of the "Update on the National Contribution to the Implementation of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change" on the afternoon of 08 February. This 2021.
The launch ceremony was attended by representatives from key ministries, institutions, development partners, NGOs, the media and the private sector. This report was prepared with the support of ministries, national institutions and partners, including: the Cambodian Climate Change Alliance (with funding from the European Union, Sweden and the United Nations Development Program), the World Bank and partners.
The Minister of Environment stated that as a country vulnerable to the effects of climate change, the Royal Government recognizes the urgency to take action to address climate change ambitiously in accordance with the Responsibilities include different levels of capacity ”under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change.
Mr. Say Samal added that Cambodia's Updated NDC report reflects the commitment and need for the next decade to achieve the vision of a low-carbon, climate-resilient and resilient society. Sustainable development.
Please be informed that this current report shows the commitment and position of the Royal Government of Cambodia to participate in tackling climate change in accordance with the Paris Agreement on Climate Change on 12 December 2015, which the Kingdom of Cambodia signed in New York, USA, April 22, 2016.
Cambodia and the international community have agreed to keep global warming below 2 degrees Celsius and continue to 1.5 degrees Celsius compared to pre-industrial levels to reduce the risks and impacts of global warming. Climate change.
