Conservationists Concerned Over Crane Presence at Anlong Pring Landscape Protection Area
(Kampot): The crane conservationist or Kok Tep said that the crane is a kind of bird, gray, long beak, bald head and it is a rare species in the world. They expressed serious concern about the presence of cranes in the Anlong Pring landscape protection area in Kampot province due to the source and lack of food in the area.
Speaking to reporters who visited the site, the crane conservationist said that in Vietnam there has been a decline in the presence of cranes until some years, cranes are not present in Vietnam due to disturbances. Habitat location and location where they forage. He added that in Thailand, cranes have become extinct, but Thailand has used the method of breeding cranes to release them back into the natural resource area.
Bou Rossak did not want to see a decrease in the presence of cranes in Cambodia as in Vietnam. He also does not want to see cranes breeding in Cambodia like in Thailand, because it takes years and costs millions of dollars, while the breeding is not easy to succeed.
This is why crane conservationists request the Royal Government to consider including Phnom Toek and Phnom Toch, which is a beautiful natural tourism area, a natural resource conservation area like Anlong Pring landscape protection area. They expect that if the area is preserved, it can attract tourists to visit and create more jobs for the people.
Anlong Pring Landscape Protection Area, with a total area of 217 hectares, was established by the Royal Government as a natural resource conservation area by Sub-Decree No. 84, dated May 09, 2016. The Anlong Pring Landscape Protected Area provides an important habitat and source of food for many migratory birds, especially cranes. It is also home to other birds, such as sea urchins, white-winged vultures, and white-striped vultures.
Phnom Toek and Phnom Toch, covering more than 600 hectares, are located near the Cambodian-Vietnamese border in Boeung Sala Khang Tbong commune and Russey commune, East district, Kampong Trach district, Kampot province. Not many people go to visit.
Mr. Bou Rossak thanked the Royal Government through the Ministry of Environment for designating the Anlong Pring Landscape Protected Area as a conservation area for cranes to live and forage. He strongly hopes that the Royal Government will consider Phnom Toek and Phnom Toch as natural resource conservation sites.
