General knowledge: Understand the history of Chinese Premier Li Keqiang
Lee's father was a former official in Hefei and also played a key role in the city's ruling party. Lee's father wanted his son to be a party leader, but he refused. Instead, Lee studied law at Peking University, earned a law degree and became chairman of the university's student council.
Li also boosted Henan's economy by turning poor areas into attractive investment destinations. In fact, Henan ranks 28th in the early 1990s to 18th in 2004.
Like Li, Xi Jinping was appointed a member of the Standing Committee of the Communist Party of China at the 17th CPC National Congress, but Xi was ahead of Li, indicating that he would be able to succeed the Chinese Communist Party secretary-general instead. Hu Jintao.
Presumably, the highest position of the Chinese Communist Party fell to Xi Jinping in the 11th National People's Congress (NPC) in March 2008, while another position at that time was Vice President of Hu's administration. Jin Tao. Li was elected deputy prime minister at the National People's Congress before becoming China's first prime minister in 2013 and second in 2018.
About privacy Li is married to Cheng Hong, an English professor at the University of Economics and Business in Beijing. His father-in-law was a former Deputy Secretary-General of the Secretariat of the Communist Youth League of the CPC Central Committee.
It should be noted that Lee and his wife got married with a daughter (studying in the United States), but this daughter was not disclosed to the public.
Mr. Ly abstains from alcohol due to regular health problems. "Lee contracted hepatitis B when he was sent to work in a rural area during the Cultural Revolution," according to a report released in Hong Kong.
Chheuteal Chrum High School Principal Sues 5 Alumni for Criticizing Posting Beer Video on Facebook, 22 Lawyers Volunteer to Defend Alumni
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Panh Putheara, Principal of Chheu Teal Chrum High School in Chheu Teal Chrum Village, Kraing Leav Commune, Bati District, Takeo Province, has decided to file a lawsuit against five former students of this high school for public defamation. The lawsuit was filed after the five alumni posted comments criticizing the beer video and posted it on Facebook.
After the video was posted, alumni of the high school came in to express their dissatisfaction with the high school's public drinking activities. In particular, he mentioned the principal of Chheuteal Chrum High School, Mr. Panh Putheara.
For these 5 alumni, they considered the principal, deputy principal and teachers of Chheuteal Chrum High School as role models for students and the general public, should set a good example in society, should not show drinking beer that society does not No encouragement.
Another student who graduated from Chheuteal Chrum High School, Khuth Sam Ath, wrote, “Private life is fun, it is fun to not hurt others. But teachers should not show such examples on social media. I apologize to all teachers, if I say something inappropriate, I will always thank you teachers.
In addition to the two alumni, three other alumni also wrote critical comments, followed by some swallows. After seeing their comments, there was also a response from some teachers in this high school, defending this video activity.
The video of the director, deputy director and some teachers drinking beer and singing entertainment posted on January 08, 2021 has not been deleted until February 6. But the comments of the five former students have been deleted after being sued by the principal.
After seeing some criticism and swallowing messages, the principal of Chheuteal Chrum High School, Mr. Panh Puthra, filed a lawsuit demanding 2 million riels from each of the five former students. Although some of them apologized for deleting the message and asked for some compensation, the high school principal refused and continued to complain to the Takeo Provincial Court.
The representative of the Takeo Provincial Prosecutor's Office, Mr. Tep Monin, summoned the five former students for questioning on February 2. However, the lawyers who volunteered to defend the five students applied for an extension.
Seeing that the principal's complaint to the five former students seemed inappropriate and awkward, more than 20 lawyers volunteered to defend the rights of the five students.
"It seems too serious, too inappropriate, so we volunteered to defend."
