Cambodia is preparing a "food safety" law to attract investors - tourists and ensure the well-being of the people
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia is busy drafting a law on "food safety" to increase protection of the well-being of the people and especially to contribute to attracting investors and tourists.
Speaking to Fresh News, Mr. Phan Oun, Director General of the General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud, Ministry of Commerce, on February 6, 2021, confirmed that this law has reached the Office of the Council of Ministers. Continue with the inter-ministerial.
Mr. Phan Oun stated that "The food safety law has reached the Office of the Council of Ministers and there will be a discussion from the Ministry of Economy and Finance in Article 29 regarding the import and export This law is important and "A lot of inter-ministerial discussions with four main ministries."
Mr. Phan Oun said that the food safety law has a lot of debate on some issues in Article 29 related to exports and imports between the Ministry of Economy and the Ministry of Commerce, and this meeting will take place on February 8. The year 2021 is coming.
He said the food safety law was important and required inter-ministerial dialogue with four main ministries: the Ministry of Commerce, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, the Ministry of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation and the Ministry of Health.
Mr. Phan Oun also stated that if the process of drafting the law in the National Assembly passes the Plenary Session of the National Assembly, the Senate and with the hand of the King, the food safety law can be completed in 2021.
At the same time, Mr. Phan Oun said that the food safety law was created to protect the health of the general public, and this law will also play an important role in contributing to economic development by attracting investors and tourists.
Mr. Phan Oun said that when this law is implemented, it will make tourists more confident about food safety in Cambodia, which makes them come to visit comfortably and Cambodia can control the safety. Food, too.
In addition, the law also contributes to attracting investment, as cross-border trade, if not regulated, makes it difficult to export goods and import goods from abroad. This is an additional emphasis of Mr. Phan Oun.
Mr. Phan Oun emphasized that the food safety law focuses only on food safety, which aims to make food safe and responsible among business people. More than 6 tons of frozen shrimp and jelly were cracked down by the authorities at the seafood shop in Mdum Trolok Bek and National Road 3 imported from Vietnam
(Phnom Penh): Officials of the General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud "KPR" in collaboration with the Phnom Penh Municipal Police have launched an operation to crack down on the import of frozen shrimp with jelly to increase the weight. To the detriment of consumers and can affect the health of a total of more than 6 tons on two vehicles. This operation took place on the night of February 6, 2021.
The operation was carried out under the direction of Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce and Chairman of the National Committee for Consumer Protection, and under the direct command of Mr. Phan Oun, Director General of the General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud. "KPR" as well as with the coordination of the prosecution.
Police confirmed that a car was intercepted along National Road 3 around the 7 Makara Dam and another car was intercepted around the Trolok Bek seafood shop. Immediately, professional officers of the General Department of the DPRK Preliminary tests on the frozen shrimp contained in the two vehicles above will find that there was a real jelly addition.
According to preliminary claims from the drivers of the two trucks, a total of 307 cases of frozen shrimp, weighing about 6 tons, were sourced through the Prek Chak International Gate and were not attached.
With the approval of the Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, the vehicle and the exhibits were sent to the General Department of KPD for further legal proceedings.
