General knowledge: If the US and the Soviets do not intervene, Korea will not be divided
1: Summary There is a direct answer that the split of the Korean Peninsula was caused by the intervention of the United States and the Socialist Republic of the Soviet Union (USSR) to end the Japanese imperial regime that ruled Korea for 35 years. . Unfortunately, although the US and Soviet powers were able to put an end to Japanese imperialism on Korean soil, the results were worse than expected, as the current situation could prove to be divided.
The cause of the divisions due to the victory over Japan occurred at the end of World War II (1939-1945), as the Cold War (1945-1991) approached as the world was torn apart. Two by American liberal ideology and Soviet communism. As the two powers seek to seize territory to expand their respective domains, Korea has fallen victim to the rift.
In a statement after the conference, Korea was the first to mention the crisis. The three powers have declared that they are paying close attention to the Korean people and are determined that Korea should gain its freedom and independence.
On August 10, 1945, two young U.S. officials were assigned to occupy Korean territory occupied by the United States. As the work was short-lived and unplanned, the two officials defined the 38th parallel as the demarcation line under US control.
The reason why the two officials chose this line is because it divides the northern and southern territories almost equally, taking Seoul as a city under US control, but at that time there were no ethnic experts. No Korea has participated in the consultation on this issue. Coincidentally, about 40 years ago, Japan and the Soviet Union also discussed dividing Korea along this line.
The division divided 16 million people under the rule of the United States and another 9 million people under the rule of the Socialist Republic of the Soviet Union. Even more surprising is that the two powers were quick to accept the split without hesitation. The agreement was reached on August 17, 1945, the day Japan raised the white flag to surrender World War II.
Post-World War II Korea situation in December 1945 at the Moscow Conference of the Allies of the Soviet Union, the United States, the Republic of China, and the United Kingdom to participate in the declaration of independence for Korea, while most Koreans demanded Immediately independent, except for the Communist Party, continued to want to be under the influence of the Soviet government.
The United States took over South Korea, with the US government fearing that the Soviet Union would expand its influence, while Japanese authorities in Korean territory warned of the risk of absenteeism, and U.S. forces invaded Korean territory. South 3 times.
The Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea, which was operating in China, sent a delegation to meet with Hoch, but he refused to meet. He also refused to recognize South Korea's new government in China and the People's Committee, saying it was illegal on December 12.
Fierce anti-Communist activist Syngman Rhee later became the first president of the South Provisional Government and later one of South Korea's most prominent politicians. The southern government launched a military campaign against leftist insurgents. In just a few years, 30,000 to 100,000 people were killed.
In 1946, the Provisional Government in the north under Soviet rule reformed the land, with all lands formerly owned by Japan and the landowners being distributed to the poor. Even large landowners were given equal land to the poor.
Land reform in North Korea is less violent than in China and Vietnam. The land reform has caused many people to flee to the south, some of whom have even taken positions in the southern government.
According to the US government, about 400,000 North Koreans have fled to the south as refugees. Major industries are considered state-owned. The economic situation in the north is more difficult than in the south. Soviet troops finally left North Korean territory in 1948.
Geneva Conference and NNSC Geneva Conference, Switzerland, was organized in 1954 in accordance with the Korean War. Despite the efforts of many countries to participate in the conference, unfortunately the event ended without a statement leading to the unification of the two Koreas.
During the peace process, a neutral National Monitoring Committee (NNSC) was formed, which was tasked with overseeing the situation in both Koreas. Since 1953, Swiss and foreign forces have been members of the NNSC, which is headquartered near the DMZ.
Although the three-year civil war ended peacefully, the North and the South continued to be enemies and always declared their legitimacy to occupy the whole of Korea, respectively. Negotiations for the unification of the two nations often end in failure.
