Most of the goods that Cambodia exports to the Thai market are agricultural products, while the products imported from Thailand to Cambodia are: cars, fuel, agricultural fertilizers, food products and beauty products.
Pen Sovicheat, Undersecretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, told Fresh News that the reasons for the decline in trade between Cambodia and Thailand in 2020 compared to 2019, we can note. As follows:
* The main reason is the blockade caused by the Kovit 19 epidemic, which disrupted trade flows between the two sides due to restrictions on entry and exit at the border, affecting both people and goods.
* The recent floods in 2020 have destroyed a large number of our agricultural products, both in quality and quantity, especially products for export to Thailand, and therefore we do not have enough goods for export.
* Market demand and purchasing power in Thailand decreased. Most of the Thai market buys Cambodian agricultural products and processes them for re-export. During the Kovid phase, the demand for Thai export markets also declined, thus making the Thai side buy less from Cambodia and seriously affecting our exports to Thailand.
"We hope that the trade balance between the two countries will improve after the vaccination and that the situation in Kovid has improved," he said.
The Prime Minister of Nepal confirmed that with this support, Cambodia has stood with Nepal to participate in the fight against Kovid-19.
He considered this gesture as a testament to the growing friendship and the continuation of the goodwill and solidarity between our two countries and peoples. We will always remember this support and goodwill.
Nepal and Cambodia have been enjoying the best relations ever since the establishment of diplomatic relations. This year marks the 45th anniversary of our diplomatic ties. Our relationship based on great will, mutual respect and mutual value has been further strengthened and expanded in recent years. This is according to the Prime Minister of Nepal.
He added, "I am pleased to recall my official visit to the beautiful country of Samdech Techo in May 2019 and Samdech Techo's visit to Nepal in November 2018. "These visits provide an opportunity for us to exchange views on a number of issues of common interest, as well as to find new ways to deepen cooperation, including in the economic field."
He added that these visits have been successful in strengthening cultural ties and close friendship between our two countries. Our culture, civilization and heritage, such as Lumpini Historical Park and Angkor Wat, remind us of our long-standing cultural ties and confirm the strong ties between our two peoples.
He believes that the existing friendly relations and cooperation between our two countries will be further strengthened and expanded in the future.
