Mr. Sun Chanthol: People with disabilities can now take the driving test
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, has announced that people with disabilities can take the driving test.
The Minister made this announcement while presiding over the inauguration ceremony of the Heavy Vehicle Driving Training Center in Prek Pnov District, Phnom Penh this morning, February 4, 2021.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol called on people with disabilities who can drive to register for a driver's license. The Senior Minister continued that he will open a special driving training course for people with disabilities.
In addition, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol stressed that people with disabilities can drive both in the country and abroad without any problems.
On that occasion, the Senior Minister and Minister of Public Works and Transport also expressed his pride that in his Ministry there is also a special parking lot and a label for people with disabilities.
Secretary of State of the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation and Secretary General of the Disability Action Council, Mr. Em Chanmakara, also joined in the celebration with this new achievement in the field of disability.
Secretary of State Em Chanmakara would like to encourage people with disabilities who have the ability to drive, please go to register for the exam to get a legal driver's license at the Ministry of Public Works and Transport.
According to an inter-ministerial statement issued in late December 2020, there are two levels of people with disabilities who can take the driving test: intermediate and advanced.
For those with moderate disability, can take the driving test and be allowed to drive, but only need to process parts of the vehicle. In particular, people with minor disabilities have the ability to take the driving test and be allowed to drive without modifying the vehicle.
The inter-ministerial declaration also specifies the types of vehicles for people with disabilities that require a driver's license. The types of vehicles for people with disabilities that require a driver's license are as follows:
* Motorcycles with a cylinder capacity of more than 125 cubic centimeters or more than 11 kilowatts of electric power. * Motorcycles, trailers, tuk-tuks, tricycles and four-wheelers. The maximum total weight of goods does not exceed 3.5 tons.
General Sem Sovanny inspects the site of an old ship that sank with unexploded ordnance in Kampong Cham province
So far, the expert team has collected more than 7 tons of unexploded ordnance, 2 human skeletons, guns, helmets, name tags and other items.
Major General Mey Sophea, Commander of the Cambodian Mine Action Center's Underwater Explosive Ordnance Disposal Unit, confirmed that the ammunition was found on a Marine ship that sank at a depth of 25 meters. To 30 meters during the Civil War, including: 60 mm, 81 mm mortars and 12.7 mm machine guns from the United States.
Major General Mey Sophea said that the ammunition and skeletons were studied by experts from the civilian era in Cambodia between 1970 and 1975 between Lon Nol and the Democratic Kampuchea Liberation Army.
"Not only were more than six tons of unexploded ordnance among the ammunition, but his special forces also collected ammunition, including helmets, knives, army badges," he said. The names of Lon Nol soldiers. ”
Major General Mey Sophea said that the munitions and human skeletons were stored by the Mine Authority in a safe deposit box near the search operation site in Meanchey commune, Srey Santhor district, Kampong Cham province, pending the Royal Government's decision. Government to destroy all these munitions.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Public Works and Transport, the staff of the Royal Government of Cambodia, this morning, Thursday, February 4, 2021, launched the first heavy vehicle training center. The center is located on National Road 5, km 18-19, Krous Village, Sangkat Samrong, Khan Prek Pnov, Phnom Penh.
The heavy vehicle driving training center is built on an area of 16,584 square meters, including 840 square meters of administrative building, 8,909 square meters of heavy vehicle driving test and examination area and accommodation for Students from the provinces are 345 square meters and equipped with many modern facilities.
This heavy vehicle driving training center is another new achievement of the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen. In particular, the presence of this training center is a contribution to reducing the rate of accidents on the road caused by unlicensed heavy truck drivers.
According to the road accident victims report, the main cause of traffic accidents is human factors up to 90%. Among those accidents are also caused by the drivers of some heavy vehicles, such as: lack of clear driving skills, did not go through proper training, do not have a driver's license or have a driver's license incorrectly according to the type of vehicle.
The report also shows that in 2019, the death toll from road accidents caused by heavy truck drivers was 181, accounting for 8.4 percent. Compared with the number of deaths caused by heavy vehicles in 2018 increased by 34 people.
Most truck drivers do not have a driver's license, and some do not have a license, depending on the type of vehicle they are driving. Especially the driver of the trailer
Mr. Chhuon Vorn, Director General of the General Department of Road Transport, said that by 2020, a total of 325,000 heavy vehicles had been registered and a "C" to "E" driving license had been issued. Weighs 218,000 cards. A comparison of vehicle registration and licensing figures shows that only 67 percent of heavy vehicle drivers have a driver's license.
In particular, about 33 percent of heavy truck drivers who are driving heavy vehicles on the road do not have a driver's license and have a valid driver's license according to the type of vehicle.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, described the establishment of the Heavy Vehicle Driving Training Center as another effort by the Royal Government of Cambodia to reduce road accidents, which is a serious concern of the Royal Government. At present.
The Senior Minister appealed to the drivers of heavy vehicles who do not yet have a driver's license to come and learn to get a valid driver's license. The senior minister appealed, "Please call on drivers who do not have a driver's license according to the type of vehicle to stop driving." If you do not have a valid driver's license, please stop and come and learn to drive properly at the heavy vehicle driving training center here.
During the ceremony, the Senior Minister also expressed his concern that some truck drivers are not old enough to comply with the law. In this regard, the Senior Minister requested all transport companies to abide by the law to select drivers who have reached the age and have a valid driver's license.
Mr. Chhuon Vorn said that the heavy vehicle driving training center took 14 months to build. The center has two parts, including:
Part 1: A building with a student reception area, administration office, library room, emergency room, meeting room, teacher room, theory room, etc.
According to the Director General of the General Department of Land Transport, the center has teachers with a master's degree, bachelor's degree and many years of experience in this field. In particular, some professional trainers have undergone vocational training from professional driving instructors in Thailand and have practiced steering at driving schools in Thailand.
As for the tuition fee, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said that the tuition fee for both theory and driving practice is $ 350, which is the state public service fee. But for the inauguration day, there is a 50% discount for the first 50 students enrolled.
The duration of the course is 30 hours of theory and 20 hours of steering in accordance with national and international standards.
The training center is equipped with modern equipment to complete the study.
The Royal Government continues to consider implementing other social assistance programs to protect poor and vulnerable families once the cash support program ends
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has been considering implementing other national programs to protect poor families and vulnerable people when the Royal Government decides to end the cash subsidy program for poor and vulnerable families. During the fight against Kovid-19 at any time.
This consideration was expressed at a meeting of the National Council for Social Protection, held on February 3, 2021, chaired by Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Economy and Finance and Chairman of the National Council for Social Protection.
The meeting also discussed and provided guidelines for consolidating the provision of health care services, as well as the preparation of pensions for the public sector that can respond to the principles that can be implemented effectively, efficiently, budgetally and ensure sustainability.
The cash aid program for poor and vulnerable families during the fight against Kovid-19 was officially launched on June 24, 2020.
According to the Ministry of Economy, as of January 2021, a total of 710,929 poor households have received allowances, including 6,421 families in Phnom Penh, 32,693 out-of-town cities and 671,815 rural households as of January 24, 2021, with an estimated 2.8 million people nationwide, have benefited from the cash subsidy program.
