(Phnom Penh): Japan was a donor to the Documentation Center of Cambodia for office supplies and building security before the trial of a senior Khmer Rouge leader.
On the afternoon of February 3, 2021, Mr. Youk Chhang, Director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, was honored to welcome Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador of Japan to Cambodia, who led his delegation, including Mr. Morinobu, First Secretary, Ms. Kusunoki Maria The third secretary and Mr. Imada Akinori, his researcher and advisor, visit the "Samdech Mey Library" of the Documentary Institute of the Documentation Center of Cambodia.
The visit begins with a detailed presentation of the exhibition of the relics of His Majesty the King Father of the Khmer Nation, His Majesty the Queen Mother of the Khmer Nation and the Royal Family, provided by Mr. Holio E. Gelres, the former biographer of the King, accompanied by music composed by the King.
Posters, exhibitions and music recall the royal love story between the King Father and the Queen Mother, which chronicles a chapter entitled "A Love That Is Not a Legend" in the book "Queen Norodom Monineath". Sihanouk, the Queen Mother of Cambodia in Freedom, Dignity and Happiness: The Royal Journey of Life is Full of Events.
This chapter deals with two songs, "Night to Meet" written by the King for the royal wedding ceremony of the two kings and the song "Monica" given to the Queen Mother to express his love. To Him.
Since the wedding, the mother-in-law has always been close to her husband's body, going through sweet and bitter memories together, as shown in the inscription. This is the story of immortal love, overcoming all obstacles and immutations that occur in his real life.
Youk Chhang then introduced the guests to the library and facilities, including digital archives, original files, photo files, video files and audio files.
Youk Chhang described in detail the long-term mission of the Documentation Center of Cambodia in promoting justice, remembrance and reconciliation through the collection of historical evidence related to genocide and crimes against humanity that the Working Group has been pursuing. .
