Tourists can travel through the town of Kulen district, through the village of Psa Chrouk to Prey Veng village, about 23 kilometers. On the other hand, it is possible to travel through Koh Ker temple to the north, through Stung Sen and through Sambor village to Prey Veng village, about 18 kilometers, the road to that area is very difficult during the rainy season.
The local Chinese temple, also known as Prey Veng, is built of five bricks facing east, built on three levels of laterite around the temple complex, with two huts, built of bricks and rooms. Eight rectangles built of sandstone and laterite, with two gopuras to the east and west of the temple, a laterite wall surrounded by a pond and baray to the east.
This Chinese temple has four ponds, two on the east and one on the west, surrounded by new walls, laterite, a structure that is rare in Khmer architecture. About 400 meters east of the temple is a large baray, 1,200 meters long and 700 meters wide.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, the temples are in the protected area, in fact, the Chinese temple is part of the complement to tourists in the Kulen-Brahma Tep Wildlife Sanctuary.
"In a natural resource protection area, not only are there rich forests and wildlife, but also cultural heritage, which is the heritage of our ancestors, should be the relevant authorities, especially the authorities," he said. The provincial tourism department should study more about the tourism potential in its province to increase new options for tourism besides Angkor Wat and Preah Vihear ...! "
According to the Chinese temple guards, the temple is not visited by many tourists due to the lack of acquaintances, as well as long distances and difficult roads.
The director of the department continued that at the Ta Suong roundabout in Kulen district, he put up a sign showing the Prey Veng community with tourist destinations that national and international visitors can visit, in addition to visiting the temple, we can also see animals.
