(Kampot): Anlong Pring Landscape Protection Area with a total area of 217 hectares was established by the Royal Government as a natural resource conservation area by Sub-Decree No. 84 Anak-Bak, dated May 09, 2016.
Located in Kampong Trach District, Kampot Province, Anlong Pring Landscape Protected Area is not only a haven for cranes, but also their tourist destination for the summer. To visit this area, if you travel from Kampot town, you have to travel to the east, a distance of more than 30 kilometers.
Currently, there are more than 70 temporary figures in the Anlong Pring landscape protection area, but this number may increase by the time the crane season arrives in the area. This is according to the contents of the provisional census, which officials at the Anlong Pring Landscape Protection Area told Fresh News Agency, which landed at this natural site.
While watching the crane, the same official said that it is even more difficult to take pictures of cranes, just find a professional photographer with a camera. Apart from that, there are only pictures taken from a magnifying glass that is placed for tourists to see the cranes and birds in the area.
According to the Ministry of Environment, cranes can only lay one or two eggs per season or per year. The mother can lay eggs for 4 to 5 weeks before the baby can hatch without any disturbance.
The crane is considered to be the highest flying bird among all the birds present on the planet. It has a red head and can live up to 80 years. Cranes are found in coastal areas in Southeast Asia and parts of northern India.
