Sok Soken: Young people around the world are at high risk of dropping out of school and work during and after Kovid-19
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sok Soken said that the majority of young people living in developing countries are likely to be unemployed or find irregular work or job arrangements that lack adequate social protection. He said young people are also at high risk of dropping out of school due to the lack of digital technology and unequal access, which leads to economic loss and opportunities.
The remarks came as Sok Soken, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation and a representative of the Cambodian People's Party in the ICAPP Youth League, attended a special video conference on "Education and Mechanism." Social support for youth during and after Kovid-19 ”organized by the ICAPP Youth Coalition and the ICAPP Secretariat on the afternoon of February 3, 2021.
It should also be noted that the purpose of the above meeting was held in a timely manner at a time when there were severe disruptions in the field of education and employment due to Kovit-19. Therefore, the leaders of political parties must take measures to prevent the long-term impact and damage to the youth.
In addition, to raise awareness of the current challenges of youth and to promote appropriate policy responses, the meeting focused on two areas:
* 1: Education: Stimulate education * 2: Employment: Expand social protection mechanisms for young people who have lost their jobs or experienced income reduction.
He also called on political parties and activists in all regions to work with the government and other stakeholders to take appropriate measures and ensure sustainable action. "In this sense, we must provide all young people with adequate health services, equal opportunities for education and employment and standard working conditions," Sok Soken said.
He added that these are important investments for young people, an indispensable part of the global workforce and a key player for social change, economic growth and technological innovation.
Mr. Sok Soken also reaffirmed the meeting's commitment to continue to do its best by monitoring the situation and taking all necessary measures to improve the well-being and livelihood of all youth and citizens.
