Offline messaging software was downloaded 600,000 times in just hours after the coup in Myanmar
(Myanmar): A popular Internet messaging company called Bridgefy says it has been downloaded more than 600,000 times in just the next few hours in Myanmar. The country's military came to power on Monday and temporarily shut down the internet. This is according to a quote from CNA on Wednesday, February 3, 2021.
The Mexican-based company, which gained popularity during the pro-democracy protests in Hong Kong in 2020, posted a message saying it hopes the people of Myanmar will be able to take advantage of the Bridgefy program "during difficult times." This.
After the arrest of Myanmar's democratically elected leaders on Monday, telephone and internet networks were cut off in Yangon and the capital, Nay Pyi Taw, as well as other areas. Although the network appears to have reopened later that night, activists in Myanmar have urged the download of the Bridgefy app as a solution in the event of a subsequent disconnection.
