(Siem Reap): An interesting model mechanism to lead and develop the tourism community for rapid growth, with the Boeung Chhouk Banteay Srei community using a mechanism to sell shares at an affordable price to villagers and outside investors to receive Invested in community development to make it more attractive.
Boeung Chhouk Banteay Srey Tourism Community is located in Banteay Srey Village, Khnar Sanday Commune, Banteay Srey District, Siem Reap Province, covering an area of 167 hectares, including 75 hectares of water. This community is one of the six model communities in Siem Reap, where the 2nd National Ecotourism Forum led participants on a study visit on 28 January 2021 to learn from successful experiences.
Mr. Phuong Savon, President of Boeung Chhouk Banteay Srei Tourism Community, said that the money used for community development is from the sale of shares to the people, especially the people in the community and the local people in Banteay Srei district, with the distribution of profits. Transparent. In this stock trading, the community has a proper management committee and has regular meetings to oversee the development of the community.
The head of the community said that since the establishment of the mechanism for the development of the way of selling shares, the community has been growing steadily and the selling price of shares has increased and the participation of more people. Has grown to more than 300 people. He said that in about six months, the community received a total profit of about $ 10,000 to distribute to each shareholder.
He added that in the community's common profit budget, community committees always share 20% as a reserve for the development of tourism products in the community.
Mr. Phuong Savon added that the community has encouraged the children of the community to join the service of tourists in the community of Boeung Chhouk Banteay Srei with a reasonable fee, which gradually improves the family life.
The establishment of this community is to participate in the conservation of local natural resources, such as forests, especially mangrove forests, freshwater wildlife, birds, especially waterfowl. And develop community-based tourism to avoid deforestation and hunting in protected areas.
Mr. Phuong Savon said that as an important experience in the Boeung Chhouk community, Banteay Srei, when tourists visit the forest protection area, the crime of destroying natural resources in the conservation area has always decreased significantly.
