(Preah Vihear): Residents in a community near the Kulen Promtep Wildlife Sanctuary in Preah Vihear province claim that they have stopped logging and hunting or trapping wildlife as before, because after earning a living Real and understand the value of Cambodia's natural resources.
Speaking to the media on the occasion of a delegation from the Ministry of Environment and development partners to study law enforcement and participation in the protection of forests and wildlife in the Kulen-Brahmapet Wildlife Sanctuary in Preah Vihear last weekend, Mr. Meas Hoeur confirmed. That per month, his members can earn from 500,000 to 600,000 riels from processing trees into various products.
According to Mr. Yim Toun Chea, Acting Community Head of Sambor Prey Veng Protected Area in Srayong Commune, Kulen District, Preah Vihear Province, more than 130 families in their community have turned to farming, such as: Implementing the Protected Rice Project Grow cassava and cashews.
He added that the business has helped people in his community cut off from subsistence on forest resources and hunting.
Mr. Yim Tuon confirmed that in this farming business, his community does not use chemicals that can harm birds such as: giant trout and other rare species. He expressed his support for protecting forest land from illegal encroachment and preventing wildlife crime in order to attract tourists to visit and bring income to his community.
On that occasion, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, said that the rice project requires member farmers to follow the main principles associated with respect for the environment, protection of natural resources, including growing rice in accordance with organic principles. Who do not use chemical fertilizers or pesticides or pesticides, do not encroach on forest land and conditions must participate in the protection and conservation of wildlife.
Similarly, Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated: "Creating a new business is an option for people to drop the old business into the forest, logging into the forest, hunting to other activities that have a regular income and do not face the law. "Some protected area communities have started to produce some processed handicrafts, sell them on the market, provide ecotourism services and participate in projects to improve their livelihoods, with the aim of reducing pressure on natural resources."
According to Mr. Net Pheaktra, "His Excellency Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment, we have a policy to provide scholarships to the children of people in the protected area community, because if we do not provide education and help to help children of "The community is highly educated, they still live in this area and do what their grandparents did."
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that for the children of the community who wish to continue their education, the Ministry will provide them with opportunities through scholarships, so we believe that the next generation of the community, once educated, know them. There will be new businesses, especially they have the knowledge to understand the importance of natural resources, this point is an important part to participate in promoting and preventing crimes against natural resources in their local area.
It should be noted that the project to plant rice and save wildlife in the Kulen Promtep Wildlife Sanctuary is being implemented jointly by the Wildlife Conservation Society of Cambodia (WCS) and USAID Prey Lang Baitong Project in collaboration with the Ministry of Environment in order to increase Livelihoods of communities and prevent encroachment on forest land from community encroachment on wildlife sanctuaries.
According to Mr. Keo Socheat, Director of Snam Mlop Prey, the community that grows this rice is a kind of romduol rice. Last harvest season, about 1,400 tons of rice was collected from these communities. The price is 1,850 riel per kilogram, which is higher than the local market price.
