Is the election promise paving the way for Min Ong Leng to become president, or does he want to follow the example of Thai Prime Minister Prayuth Chan-o-cha by resigning from the military to lead a civilian government?
* Ambition to be president?
Under the 2008 constitution, Myanmar's military automatically holds 25 percent of the seats in parliament, or 166 seats, without having to go through an election, and the pro-military USDP has to win another 167 seats to nominate Min Aung Leng. "President." However, the party won only 33 seats in the 498-seat parliament, while Suu Kyi's National League for Democracy won 396 seats.
* Tough decisions
She said the existing quota seats were automatically in the hands of the Myanmar military to veto any constitutional amendment. But their positions or roles may be weakened when they do not have a majority to oppose or pass other laws, which will pose challenges to the military's position and power in Myanmar.
On the other hand, while Min Aung Leng successfully staged a coup, observers see that there are still many questions about his ability and the military to maintain power. In a statement released on Monday, the National League for Democracy (NLD) said Aung San Suu Kyi had called on all Myanmar people not to recognize the coup and to stand up and protest. .
After the military coup, Myanmar imposed a "one-year state of emergency" along with a curfew from 8 pm to 6 am. The military has also announced that 24 of Suu Kyi's ministers and deputy ministers have been removed and 11 have been reappointed in the Ministry of Finance, Health, Interior, Foreign Affairs and Information. Original.
