The American brought his family to live in the jungles of Cambodia in 2012 after leaving his homeland in 1992 to work as a rural development and humanitarian expert on clean water, wells, toilets. After 20 years in Cambodia, it was only during 2012 that he applied to live in Phnom Tnaot, Phnom Pok.
Although crime has been steadily declining in protected wildlife sanctuaries, Ben has expressed concern over a more prudent act of trapping by hunters, who have set traps in the area. The thick forest that made it difficult for him, as well as park rangers and the community, to reach and remove the traps.
"Wildlife and forests are very important to all of us, for the Cambodian people as well as for tourism," said the foreign environmental activist. "So I need to come in and defend this area."
Meanwhile, Ms. Sarin, the wife of Mr. Ben Davis, said that this place serves food, natural guesthouse for tourists to visit. In the past, many tourists, especially Cambodians, have visited the forest and wildlife in this place.
"If you come here, you have to tell me first by phone or Facebook because there is a reception limit. It is not possible to get too much here," Sarin said. I have prepared vegetarian food for them. She also described that in addition to looking at the forest and wildlife, this place also offers a rope ride from the brown mountain down to see the beautiful scenery of mountains and waterfalls.
