A group of 10 hippos armed with knives cut a young man seriously injured in Ang Snoul district, police are searching for the perpetrator
(Central): A young man was attacked by a group of about 10 hippos, armed with knives and samurai swords in his arms, seriously injured and almost died while the victim was having a drink with friends in a barn at Chlung Mlou resort. In Chong Boeung village, Makak commune, Ang Snoul district, Kandal province.
This incident happened on January 30, 2021, which caused a victim named Phum Samuth, 20 years old, living in Chong Boeng village, Makak commune, Ang Snoul district, while the unidentified perpetrators escaped after the incident. .
Local police confirmed that prior to the incident, the victim had visited the Chunlong Mlou resort with four other female friends, including Chet Niti, Phal Srey Leak, 17-year-old Thea Socheata and Sor Srey Leak. .
Later, Phal Srey Leak, Thea Socheata and Sor Srey Leak sat in different quarters, while Phum Samut and Chet Niti sat in two quarters. Later, a group of about 10 people on a motorcycle with knives and swords cut the victim, causing serious injuries and escaped.
Relatives then took the victim to Art Hospital in Andong Kravien village, Makak commune, and took him to Lok Sang Hospital for emergency treatment. So far, the specialized police forces of the districts and provinces are searching for relevant subjects to be punished according to the law. Myanmar: From Democracy to Military
Myanmar's military on Monday seized power after the arrest of Aung San Suu Kyi and several other senior government officials. But for the people of this Southeast Asian nation, it is not uncommon for their country to be under military rule.
The following are the main events that have taken place in Myanmar:
Aung San Suu Kyi, the leader of the British independence movement, was assassinated. Aung San Suu Kyi is Aung San's only daughter.
Myanmar officially gained independence from Britain under former President U Nu.
The Myanmar military, led by General Ne Win, came to power in a coup. All opposition parties at the time were banned and the military controlled all trade and industry that sank the national economy due to international sanctions.
Pro-democracy demonstrations erupted in August and were violently suppressed by the military, according to civil society groups, with some 5,000 people killed in the crackdown. In September of the same year, Aung San Suu Kyi formed the National League for Democracy (NLD).
Under international pressure, the Myanmar military staged an election in which Suu Kyi's party won a landslide victory. But the military refused to recognize the election results and even arrested Suu Kyi under house arrest.
In July, Aung San Suu Kyi was released from house arrest.
Myanmar joins the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, or ASEAN.
Aung San Suu Kyi has been again under house arrest by the military, prompting Europe to increase sanctions on Myanmar.
Aung San Suu Kyi has been released again and is allowed to travel around the country.
Aung San Suu Kyi has been re-arrested by the military, claiming to protect her safety after an attack on her convoy.
The Myanmar military has announced that it has decided to move the capital to Nay Pyi Taw. Yangon was originally the capital of Myanmar.
Thousands take to the streets in Yangon after the military government cut off fuel aid. Dozens of people were reported killed in the military crackdown.
The transition to democracy began with controversy surrounding a referendum on a constitutional amendment held just two days after Cyclone Nargis hit Myanmar.
The USDP, close to the Myanmar military, won an election in which the NLD boycotted. Aung San Suu Kyi was released from house arrest in November of the same year.
The NLD won a landslide victory in the general election, and Suu Kyi became the leader with the role of State Councilor of Myanmar.
Conflict in Rakhine State has erupted after the Myanmar military claimed that Rohingya militants had killed nine police officers. The violence has forced the death of hundreds of Rohingya and forced hundreds of thousands to flee to Bangladesh, Malaysia and Indonesia.
The Myanmar military has led a military operation in Rakhine state, forcing more than 730,000 Rohingya to flee to Bangladesh. UN investigators have accused the Myanmar military of involvement in genocide against the Rohingya. But her government representative, Aung San Suu Kyi, has denied the allegations.
Armed clashes erupted in Rakhine state between the Myanmar military and Rohingya militants. Suu Kyi also urged the military to crack down on the rebels.
January 28 - The Election Commission dismisses military allegations, saying the election was transparent and fair.
January 30 - The Myanmar military says it will uphold the constitution and the rule of law.
