Monday's unrest, which some observers see as a "coup", erupted over a dispute over the results of the November 8, 2020 election, which showed her National League for Democracy (NLD) party. Suu Kyi won a landslide victory with 83 percent of the vote, or 396 of the total seats in parliament.
The Myanmar National Election Commission (NEC) has declared that the election was transparent and fair, and the United States and the European Union have urged the military to recognize the results. However, Myanmar's military chief, Min Aung Leng, accused the election of being rigged and warned that it would take action.
Who is in charge of Myanmar?
Aung San Suu Kyi, a 75-year-old Nobel Peace Prize winner and figure-hater in the West, has become a leader and adviser to Myanmar after her party won a landslide victory. Land in the 2015 general election after she was under house arrest for decades (from 1989 to 2010).
Under the current constitution, Myanmar is governed by a power-sharing mechanism between Suu Kyi's civilian government and Min Aung-leng's military, with the automatic quota of 25 percent of parliamentary seats for the military.
The Myanmar National Election Commission (NEC) has declared that the election was transparent and fair, and the United States and the European Union have urged the military to recognize the results. According to the latest election results, Suu Kyi's party won 83 percent of the parliamentary seats, while the pro-military Union Solidarity and Development Party (USDP) won only 33 of the 476 seats.
How did Suu Kyi's party and other political parties react to the election?
Myanmar's military spokesman Brigadier General Zaw Min Tun said at a press conference last week that the Myanmar military would take action in response to the fraudulent election and would use all available means. Includes Supreme Court resolution.
What does the Myanmar Constitution say about this issue?
Myanmar's current constitution states that the military commander-in-chief can only seize power and lead the country in the event of possible national disintegration, disunity and loss of sovereignty.
Last week, Min Ong Leng said that the law and the constitution must be obeyed, but if the law is not implemented or obeyed, it should be repealed or amended.
* International reaction to the arrest of Aung San Suu Kyi
Guterres also called on the military to respect the election results and the will of the people of Myanmar, while the United States warned that it would retaliate if the Myanmar military refused to release Suu Kyi and other detainees. Singapore and Japan have also expressed concern over the situation in Myanmar, calling on all parties concerned to exercise restraint.
