This Morning, Samdech Techo Presides Over the Groundbreaking Ceremony for a Water Treatment Plant in Phnom Penh and Takhmao City
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on Monday morning, February 1, 2021, continued to open the groundbreaking ceremony and inaugurate new achievements for the nation and the people of Cambodia. The second public appearance of Samdech Techo Hun Sen at the beginning of 2021 is the opening ceremony of the construction site of Bak Kheng Water Treatment Plant and the inauguration ceremony of Chamkar Mon Water Treatment Plant.
The water treatment plant will increase water production capacity by 70% throughout Phnom Penh and Takhmao, in addition to the current water production capacity from 600,000 cubic meters per day to 1 million cubic meters per day. In 2025.
Cambodia, devastated by the genocidal regime of Pol Pot, after the liberation on January 7, 1979, had no clean water system in Phnom Penh. Not to mention that ordinary people, even the national leaders at that time, had to go down to fetch water and carry water from the river to use.
Water is a policy of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which is also led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen. For more than 40 years, along with the efforts to build and develop the country, the Royal Government has also made efforts to expand the water supply system for the people to use.
However, this water supply has faced a sharp increase in demand for clean water since the beginning of 2010. This increase was due to the construction activities of high-rise buildings as well as the increase in population.
He said that the third phase of the master plan has identified a large investment program with a budget of about 500 million US dollars in order to increase water production capacity in addition to the current water production capacity.
Mr. Sim Sitha said that this project to expand the capacity of Chamkar Mon Water Treatment Plant is the first project of the third phase of the master plan, which was completed in October 2019.
Separately, Bak Kheng Water Supply Project is the second project of the third phase of the master plan. When studying the feasibility report in 2017, the project was located in Sangkat Bak Kheng, 10 km north of the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge (Chroy Changva Bridge ).
The construction process of this plant will be carried out in two parallel steps and it is planned to launch a test run in the production capacity of half water (nearly 200,000 cubic meters per day) in the first quarter of 2023 and will be fully operational in both stages. Nearly 400,000 cubic meters per day) in the first quarter of 2024.
The European Union (EU) grant funding refers to the expansion of water supply to low-income communities living in some areas on the outskirts of Phnom Penh.
