New South Korean Ambassador to Cambodia urges Phnom Penh administration to encourage and support Korean companies to invest in waste recycling in Phnom Penh (Video inside) (Phnom Penh) Phnom Penh encourages and supports Korean companies to invest in recycling waste into electricity, oil, gas and gas.
This statement was made on the occasion of the Ambassador of the Republic of Korea paid a courtesy call on Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh on the afternoon of January 28, 2021 at Phnom Penh Capital Hall.
Mr. Park Geun-hye said that the Korean investment company has asked for investment by recycling waste into electricity, oil, gas and gas. No trash yet.
On this issue, the Ambassador asked the Phnom Penh administration to support and encourage Korean companies to invest in recycling. He said that the company has a lot of experience in recycling waste by heating garbage up to 400 degrees Celsius and without harming the environment.
Regarding the above request for support, Mr. Khuong Sreng stated that the Phnom Penh Capital Administration has always supported the investment of waste recycling of Korean companies that wish to invest in waste in Phnom Penh.
He said that the important thing is for the company to show its capacity so that the relevant ministries can see the company's ability, the company will receive the right to invest from the government. For the administration of Phnom Penh will always support the investment of Korean companies, because the garbage in Phnom Penh has not been processed yet, both landfills in Stung Meanchey and in Dangkor.
In the project, Phnom Penh Capital Hall plans to build a new and large landfill in Tuol Prich Village, Street 51, covering an area of more than 100 hectares to dump garbage there. So after the Korean company has invested in this waste, in Phnom Penh, there is no shortage of waste for recycling. He said that for Phnom Penh, if all garbage is collected in a day, not less than 4,000 tons of garbage.
