(Mondulkiri): A male white-tailed monkey, an endangered species, was voluntarily handed over to the Ministry of Environment for further protection and conservation. The two-month-old male white-tailed monkey will be cared for, protected and raised by conservation groups so that the monkey can be healthy and self-controlled before being released back into the wild.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, in 2020, two white-tailed macaques were handed over to the Mondulkiri provincial law enforcement authorities and are being cared for by conservationists.
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra stressed that "Please a small number of people who are still harassing wildlife must stop hunting, trapping and trapping wild animals and stop raising rare animals, otherwise they will face The law.
According to a study by wildlife officials, the white-tailed macaque is the world's most endangered species. There are no reports of total white-tailed monkeys in the world, but there are not many left. According to the data, there are currently more than 20,000 white-tailed macaques in Cambodia, and the highest number of white-tailed macaques is present in the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri province, with a total of 6,756 groups.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra pointed out that with good conservation and strict law enforcement in the last 10 years, the number of white-tailed monkeys in Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary has been stable. In addition to the white-tailed macaque, Cambodia also has other endangered species in the world, such as: yellow-cheeked vulture, coyote monkey, crab monkey, crab monkey and so on.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that in the wildlife conservation efforts, the Ministry of Environment and partner organizations, together with the protected area community, have educated people not to eat wildlife meat and to prevent wildlife crime and remove traps from the area. Natural blessings.
