Flood Preparedness Center in Kampong Svay District, Kampong Thom Province Plan to Complete Construction Soon
(Kampong Thom): The Royal Government of Japan and the World Food Program (WFP) have joined forces with the National Committee for Disaster Management to support the construction of a safe shelter at Wat Khsach Chiros for the flood season in Kampong Svay district, Kampong Thom province, which is scheduled to end. Coming soon.
On that occasion, Mr. Sok Lou said that on behalf of the local authorities of the 11 communes in Kampong Svay district are also the whole Cambodian people, I would like to express my deepest gratitude for the national level, always pay attention to the sub-national level In all cases of natural disasters, and especially the World Food Organization and the government, donors (Japan) support in all areas.
The provincial governor confirmed that 85% of the people are engaged in agriculture, farming, 10% fishing, 5% selling labor. In response to the previous floods, the number of families who used to evacuate was 350, equivalent to 1,518 people. Difficulties, lack of safe places to bring people into the pagoda, affecting the monks and lack of toilets, lack of clean water to eat and use, lack of food and so on.
On that occasion, Mr. Mikami Masahiro and Ms. Yukonan, Director, also expressed their happiness and support for the project to build a safe evacuation center with the generous support of the Government of Japan through the Disaster Management Committee and in collaboration with the World Food Program. Establish strong partnerships at both the national and sub-national levels to reduce the impact of disasters on flood-prone communities.
On behalf of Senior Minister Kun Kim, Senior Minister, I would like to express my deep gratitude to the Ambassador of Japan to Cambodia and highly commend the Government of Japan for its traditional partners and long-standing friends who have always been attentive and helpful. The Cambodian people not only provide structural projects but also provide emergency assistance to help all Cambodians affected by the rapid floods in 2020.
Mr. Hang Samoeun said that this is a joint project called "Basic Development Project for Disaster Preparedness to Improve the Living Environment in Cambodia" under the five-year national strategic plan (20-2023). Of the World Food Program (WFP), including about $ 2.7 million to reduce risk and improve disaster response preparedness in line with the Royal Government's Disaster Management Policy.
