* Continue to promote cooperation, partnership and dialogue between recipients and donors at all levels to strengthen trust and closer ties. Minister Say Sam Al: More than 20 nature protection communities and protected areas are active in tourism
(Siem Reap): Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment, said that the Ministry of Environment has established 174 local communities in protected areas throughout Cambodia, of which more than 20 protected communities and nature reserves are engaged in tourism activities.
"Among these, there are more than 20 protected area communities and other communities in protected areas that have the potential for eco-tourism and are providing this service to tourists," he said.
The Minister said that the development of ecotourism based on the principles of environmental sustainability as well as the active participation of local people and the equitable distribution of economic benefits not only contribute to improving the livelihood of communities and local economic development, but It also contributes significantly to environmental protection, natural resource management, biodiversity conservation and sustainable development.
In addition, the Ministry of Environment always pays attention to support them in the long run by promoting the implementation of state policies, strategies and plans, such as ecotourism, modern agriculture, community health, clean water and nutrition. Provide immediate intervention, especially during the Kovid-19 crisis, and the provision of vegetable and animal varieties.
He proposed some suggestions to address some of the challenges and promote community-based tourism and ecotourism, including:
* Relevant ministries and institutions should establish or strengthen additional coordination and cooperation mechanisms to provide support for the promotion of physical infrastructure and other public services to local communities in accordance with the state's multi-sectoral policies, such as clean water. Toilets, health, solid waste management, urban wastewater, etc.
* Relevant ministries and institutions should continue to coordinate and prepare various legal documents to create a better environment for the livelihood of the community.
* All stakeholders are encouraged to continue to promote community capacity building through vocational training, entrepreneurship, technical outreach, and the necessary material and resource support, especially on agriculture and animal husbandry.
* All stakeholders should continue to coordinate and promote the establishment of marketing networks, savings groups and production groups in the local community, etc. to ensure the value chain and market sustainability for the community.
* NGOs and development partners are encouraged to provide both financial and technical support to local communities in complement to the Royal Government's policies and intervention measures to ensure consistency and high efficiency. Police Continue to Crack Down on Illegal Fishing in Koh Sdech Kmeng Canal, Krakor District, Pursat Province
(Pursat): The Anti-Economic Crime Police Force cooperated with the Krakor Fisheries Administration and Sangkat of Krakor Fisheries Administration, the District Police Inspector, Village and Sub-Village Chiefs, the Community and the People's Defense Force led by Lt. Col. Meas Bunthoeun, Deputy Commissioner of the Provincial Police. Pursat has continued to crack down on fishing offenses at the border of Krakor district, Pursat province and Boribo district, Kampong Chhnang province.
This operation took place on January 27, 2021 at the point of Samdech Kmeng canal, geography of Kampong Thdol village, Ansa Chambok commune, Krakor district, Pursat province, up to the border of Boribo district, Kampong Chhnang province, which is the main waterway for fish.
According to Lieutenant Colonel Meas Bunthoeun, Deputy Commissioner of Pursat Provincial Police, this operation was carried out with the coordination of Mr. Koy Kanya, Prosecutor of the Provincial Prosecutor's Office and under the direct command of Mr. Mao Thor. Nin, Chairman of the Provincial Unity Command.
Lt. Col. Meas Bunthoeun confirmed that during this operation, the specialized force confiscated 16 nets, 6,000 meters, 17 nets, 3 nets, 830 wooden poles and released a variety of fish. 105 kg and dismantled 30 branches in each place with a size of 30 meters by 30 meters.
The exhibits were destroyed on the spot, while the suspects have been targeted by specialized forces and are being brought to justice.
