Cambodia to launch commercial and labor courts this year to boost investor confidence
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia is studying to launch two specialized courts, the Commercial Court and the Labor Court, in 2021 to ensure quality, fairness and to increase investor confidence.
Recently, Mr. Keut Rith, Minister of Justice, also issued two separate resolutions on the establishment of the Commercial and Labor Court Organizing Committees in order to study and prepare the two specialized courts.
Mr. Chin Malin said that the purpose of establishing these two specialized courts is to ensure the quality of justice in the case process, resolve cases and also participate in speeding up the process and reducing congestion.
In addition, to increase investor confidence by improving the quality of case management services in each profession, which contributes to reducing congestion through faster court proceedings. This is an additional emphasis of Mr. Jean Malin.
Mr. Jean Malin added that the establishment of this specialized court to facilitate as well as improve the quality of justice through the handling of cases, and each case will be resolved by the court and the judge on each special case.
At the same time, a spokesman for the Ministry of Justice confirmed that the two specialized courts are not separate from the specialized courts, they are in the same ordinary courts, only divided into specialized courts.
Mr. Malin also said that although Cambodia did not have a commercial and labor tribunal before, this did not mean that labor and trade cases were not settled. Civil.
ARDB CEO asks farmers to change their mindset from rice to barns for rice to rice to make a profit as an entrepreneur
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kao Thach, the Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the Rural Development Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (ARDB), asked the farmers to change their mindset from rice to barn for a year to be sold. Selling for a living as an entrepreneur is not the same as farming as a normal farmer.
The proposal was made during an invitation to a seminar and dissemination of market-related credit principles to a total of nearly 300 farmers growing safe vegetables in Siem Reap.
Mr. Kao Thach requested, "Please change your habit of farming as a farmer to cultivate as an entrepreneur, that is, to cultivate for business by knowing how to plan income and expenditure clearly, have a plan to supply to the market." With quality, safety and sustainability.
At the same time, he wants to encourage people to cultivate to replace the imported from abroad without sources and to encourage exports. As a result, your life will change and prosper.
Mr. Kao Thach stated that the purpose of this round is to introduce farmers to the Khmer Safe Vegetable Market Company and encourage farmers to sign agricultural contracts with this company so that they have a clear market and guarantee the actual price. .
He confirmed that the Khmer Safe Vegetable Market Company was established with the support of the Rural Development Bank and Agriculture, which is a state-owned bank, and with the participation of business people in the private sector. The company has the role of collecting safe vegetables from farmers to wholesale and retail.
He asked farmers to consider signing agricultural contracts with safe vegetable market companies to supply vegetables to the market, and especially farmers will stop worrying about market issues and prices. Through this agricultural contract, your farm will ensure both price and market sustainability because we have real buyers, real markets and real payments without causing you any problems.
Mr. Kao Thach continues to encourage farmers to contact ARDB in case they need credit to expand their cultivation and business.
He continued to encourage farmers to select quality varieties for cultivation, and the cultivation must have a clear and clear planting plan to supply the market regularly and stably. Here, he stressed that in the past, people planted without a plan, causing a lot of losses or profits due to inconsistent supply to the market. Sometimes cultivated products flood the market, causing prices to fall.
