(Phnom Penh): Dr. In Channy, President and CEO of ACLEDA Bank, said that the use of electronic payment systems now provides a lot of convenience and benefits to the banking sector.
In fact, ACLEDA receives $ 1 billion in electronic payments per day. On the other hand, the number of customers using the service at all bank branches is decreasing.
Dr. In Channy said, "ACLEDA Bank's daily transfer fund is not less than $ 1 billion for 2020 and this is the highest growth." Consumers using electronic payment systems are growing. In the past, there were a lot of customers using ACLEDA Bank's counters in all branches.
"For 2020, only 18% of the more than 2 million customers who use ACLEDA Bank's counters will use electronic payment via mobile phone," he said.
Dr. N. Channy added that for 2021, the use of customers' stands is less than 12 percent or 10 percent, because now the use of electronic systems is easy, fast, costly and less time. Therefore, customers do not need to come to the counter, when customers come to the counter, their expenses are profitable, while the cost of ACLEDA Bank is less.
US provides $ 420,000 for the renovation of Phnom Bakheng and Preah Vihear temples
(Phnom Penh): The United States Government has provided $ 420,000 to the Ambassador for Cultural Preservation for the restoration of parts of Phnom Bakheng Temple in Siem Reap and Preah Vihear Temple in Preah Vihear Province to contribute to the preservation of valuable temples. Of Cambodia. This is according to an official from the US Embassy in Cambodia who told Fresh News on January 26.
"These historical sites are an enduring part of Cambodia's unique cultural heritage that is recognized and valued around the world," the ambassador said.
Ms. Phoeung Sakona said that this contribution underscores the long-standing support and strong commitment of the United States Government, together with the international community, in the protection, preservation and restoration of Cambodia's cultural heritage. Relations and cooperation based on common values between Cambodia and the US government and people will continue to grow stronger.
According to the US Embassy, the Ambassador's Fund for Cultural Preservation (AFCP) was set up to help preserve important cultural heritage sites. Under the auspices of this fund, the United States Department of State has provided more than $ 4 million to Cambodia since 2001. The United States Government has also provided financial assistance for the preservation of other important cultural heritage sites in Cambodia.
The US Embassy expressed the hope that through the US-Cambodia Partnership Project, the heritage will be boosted by tourism and a better economy.
