(Alaska): A new study shows that the amount of ice on Earth today is experiencing a much faster rate of melting than in the mid-1990s. This was released by CNA on Tuesday morning, January 26, 2021.
About 28 trillion tonnes of ice have melted on Earth since the mid-1990s, according to a new study compiled by scientists in the UK and released on Monday. At annual rates, the current melting point is 57 percent faster than nearly three decades ago.
Using satellite data from 1994 to 2017, the scientists calculated that the world lost an average of 0.8 trillion tons of ice per year during the 1990s, but during the last few years. Since then, the earth has lost an average of 1.2 trillion tons of ice per year.
Many other recent studies by scientists around the world have also found alarming rates of melting glaciers as well as global warming. .
