EU and Cambodian Entrepreneurs Support Partnership for Clean Energy Innovation in Agriculture
(Phnom Penh): Cambodian Entrepreneurs, an institution under the Ministry of Economy and Finance (MEF) and EnergyLab, the only start-up business trainer in Cambodia's clean energy sector, have signed a partnership agreement to support Innovative clean energy business in agriculture, fisheries and aquaculture in Cambodia with EU grant.
This partnership aims to promote the business ideas of clean energy entrepreneurs in developing competitive products that meet the energy needs of the Tonle Sap agribusiness enterprise. EnergyLab recruits 5-10 clean energy start-ups per year to participate in a one-year start-up training program to guide those businesses with intensive training, one-on-one guidance and consulting.
Cambodian entrepreneurs will provide "initial investment assistance" to start-ups selected for product testing, sales, marketing and business development strategies.
Dr. Chheang Vann Monin, Executive Director of Cambodian Entrepreneurs, said that with the support of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, Cambodian entrepreneurs have a vision to become a national nursery for the promotion of entrepreneurship in Cambodia.
"Our support will encourage Cambodian entrepreneurs to develop new green technologies and create business solutions for agriculture, aquaculture and fisheries," said Carmen Moreno, EU Ambassador to Cambodia. "Innovation using clean energy in the value chain can help rural businesses expand their businesses, strengthen competitiveness and pave the way for economic recovery based on green technology."
The Clean Energy Innovation Business Training Program in Agriculture and Aquaculture supports the first seven entrepreneurs who are developing businesses to increase aquaculture and agricultural production and optimize post-harvest costs, such as refrigeration or solar drying. A selected startup is also working on solar panels to increase the productivity of fish farms.
