(Phnom Penh): Ms. Youk Sambath, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, stated that as expected, the partner organizations as well as the World Health Organization have cooperated with Cambodia to reduce the new rate of hepatitis B and C virus infections by up to 90%. And a mortality rate of 65% by 2030.
Through the National Strategic Plan, Cambodia is committed to working with partner organizations to achieve the elimination of hepatitis B and C from further outbreaks that pose a threat to public health.
This statement was made by Ms. Youk Sambath at the consultation meeting on the implementation of the National Strategic Plan and the financing for the Hepatitis B Response Program (B and C 2020-2024) held on 22 January 2021. Tonle Bassac 2 Restaurant.
"The Ministry of Health also considers the hepatitis B and C response to be one of the top priorities, so I would like to encourage joint efforts to further strengthen research to achieve this," she said. "The goal is to eradicate hepatitis by 2030."
She said people living with hepatitis C also received treatment through the National Center for AIDS, Dermatology and Sexually Transmitted Infections (NCHADS), which is funded by the Global Fund. However, the nationwide response to the treatment of both hepatitis occurred only in national hospitals and some private services, not yet widely covered, especially at the sub-national level across the country.
The Cambodian Ministry of Health is committed to working with partners to address the risks associated with hepatitis B and C, focusing on the vision: “There is no new hepatitis B infection in Cambodia where people with chronic hepatitis B virus "Access to diagnostic, treatment and care services that are effective, affordable and of good quality."
To achieve this vision, the Ministry of Health, which has the Department of Infectious Diseases (CDC) as its body, has set up a technical working group for liver disease, which was formally established in early 2019 to address issues related to hepatitis due to the virus. In the country.
For members of the Technical Working Group, including relevant institutions, the World Health Organization, NGOs and development partners, who have been actively involved in the consultation, consultation and preparation of the National Strategic Plan for Hepatitis C in Cambodia 2020. 2024 Guidelines for the care and treatment of hepatitis B and C carriers approved by the Ministry of Health.
