(Takeo): Vendors at Chea Sim High School in Daun Keo City, Takeo Province, signed a letter to Daun Keo Mayor Kang Anan for help after the high school principal threatened to dismantle their stalls. They bought a new stall for nearly $ 2,000.
According to a letter to the Daun Keo mayor, Chea Sim High School principal Try Chhay recently convened a meeting of vendors and told them to dismantle their stalls and to buy new ones. $ 1,734, with a 50% down payment required for only three days. If you refuse to pay, the school will not allow you to sell or dismantle the stall.
Vendors have asked the Daun Keo mayor to intervene to allow them to sell at the same house, as his economic situation is deteriorating due to the 19-year-old Kovid disease, which has prevented them from selling for almost a year. He could not afford to raise $ 1,754 to buy a new stall.
"If there is a letter requesting such intervention, I will investigate the actual location," he said. Lor Virith, director of the Takeo Provincial Department of Education, Youth and Sports, could not be reached for comment today regarding the construction of a stall at the school. The Royal Government decided to cancel the title deed to the pine plantation land in Oreang district and Sen Monorom city and to issue the confiscation order back to the state property
(Phnom Penh): Finally, at the request of Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, the Royal Government of Cambodia has decided to cancel all letters issued by the authorities of villages, communes, districts and provinces in accordance with the previous procedures. And issued an order to confiscate these lands as state property for the pine plantation land in Oreang district and Sen Monorom city, Mondulkiri province.
This decision is presented in a letter to the Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and the Governor of Mondulkiri on January 21, 2021.
The Office of the Council of Ministers also decided that the provincial administration should prepare an action plan to launch a large-scale campaign to clear all forms of illegal encroachment and encroachment on state land, with the joint support of national forces.
The Office of the Council of Ministers also decided that the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries should examine the possibility of requesting regulation or filing a lawsuit for annulment of immovable property certificates that have not been completed in full force in force.
Mr. Veng Sakhon added that in some cases, the land was violated by the local authority, and some places even had a title deed to the real estate. At the same time, the natural forest area protected by the valleys and streams in the economic land concessions of the two companies has also been illegally encroached upon.
The Minister of Agriculture further described to Samdech Techo that in addition to the pine forest and natural forest in the two economic land concession companies, there is also illegal logging, clearing and fencing of state-owned pine plantations and land. The pine plantation, which was planted by His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of the Kingdom of Cambodia, during the 2005 National Tree Day Ceremony.
Mr. Veng Sakhon emphasized that some of the locations that were invaded had a certificate of ownership of the property and a title deed.
According to information from the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, in 2005 the Royal Government granted the right to invest in Wujisan Company with an area of 10,000 hectares to plant pine trees, vegetables and fruits and processing plants. In 2010, the Royal Government granted the right to invest in Hua Ling Company with an area of 8,400 hectares to plant pine trees.
Mr. Veng Sakhon claimed that both companies have been planting pine cones in a row and so far the planted pine trees can be harvested as well. But the area under the pine plantation has been illegally occupied by many hectares, by encroachment on a small group of people, violating legal rights without fear of the law and investment companies.
Please note that the encroachment on state land as personal property is not new for Mondulkiri. In 2020, there was also a review of land encroachment cases in Oreang and Keo Seima districts. At that time, the people involved in issuing the land purchase and sale documents were fined, and the governor of Koh Nhek district, Mr. Sin Vanvuth, was also removed from office.
