Elderly people receive training on human rights and priorities from the Cambodian Human Rights Committee
(Battambang): The Cambodian Human Rights Committee, in collaboration with the Cambodian Organization for the Elderly, has launched a training course on "Human Rights and Priorities for the Elderly" with 45 members of the Cambodian Elderly Network representing 25,867 of the 282 elderly. Associations from 5 capitals and provinces: Kampong Thom, Siem Reap, Banteay Meanchey, Battambang and Phnom Penh.
The opening ceremony of the training course on "Human Rights and Priorities for the Elderly" was held for two days from 19 to 20 January 2021, chaired by Mr. Keo Remy, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister. And Chairman of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee at Battambang Provincial Hall.
This training aims to build the capacity of older people to understand and gain knowledge about global human rights, laws and interventions in Cambodia, as well as to discuss the needs of older people in relation to human rights.
During the two-day training, participants expressed strong interest in topics related to human rights and freedoms and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, as well as the state's obligation to protect human rights through participation and Many questions around all these important topics.
On this occasion, the President of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee also mentioned the importance of establishing a mechanism for community-based elderly associations, which is an important contribution to supporting the elderly in need. Shelter and live alone and support the promotion of the Convention on the Rights of the Elderly, as well as all activities that support and promote the well-being of the elderly in Cambodia.
At the same time, Mr. Keo Remy also promised to bring all this work to the top leadership, including Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, as well as promised to help communicate to other development partners.
Mr. Keo Remy also expressed his support and praise to the Cambodian Organization for the Elderly as a model organization that cares and does a lot of work to contribute to the well-being of the elderly in Cambodia.
In addition, Mr. Keo Remy also called on the relevant ministries, provincial authorities, relevant departments to continue to cooperate and support the organizations and associations of the elderly for the benefit of the elderly and the next generation. Find old age in the future.
Oum Narin, a member of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, said at the closing ceremony that all citizens have equal rights, but remember that the exercise of individual rights must be limited by the laws of each country. Cambodia Under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Cambodian people have equal rights and freedoms, and all the rights that the Cambodian people enjoy now come because Cambodia is peaceful.
Mr. Oum Narin also conveyed a message from the President of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee to his uncle, aunt and all the brothers and sisters, when he returned home, please keep the distance by following the health rules to prevent Kovid-19 disease, because uncle and aunt They are elderly, so they are very susceptible to the disease because Battambang, a province bordering Thailand, has recently found Cambodian workers returning from Thailand with Kovid-19 disease.
In addition, he also reminded all senior members to advise their children who have just returned from abroad to go to the health center for 14 days to ensure that they are free of Kovid. 19.
Ms. Ou Vanda, 64 years old, President of the Cambodian Network for the Elderly, expressed her personal interest that she was excited to participate and gain knowledge about human rights, especially to understand and discuss the priorities as well as the needs. The importance of the elderly around all these rights.
She also expressed great pleasure to hear that Mr. Keo Remy, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, promised to support the push for a new Convention on Old Human Rights.
In particular, Nhek Saran, 69, was happy to participate and gain knowledge about human rights and priorities for the elderly. He also expressed his pride at hearing the words of the President of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee pledging to support the activities of the Association of the Elderly in the work to improve the welfare of the elderly in Cambodia.
Please note that the Cambodian Elderly Network is a forum for the Association of the Elderly, NGOs and institutions to discuss and work together to improve the quality of life of the elderly and contribute to the implementation of policies. Effectiveness that is beneficial for the elderly.
(Mondulkiri): Mr. Seng Teak, President of WWF, compared Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary like unprocessed diamonds for eco-tourism to attract foreign tourists to stay longer, then visit the ancient temples in Siem Reap. Continue to visit natural forest areas that are rich in wildlife.
Mr. Seng Teak made this comparison on the occasion of accompanying Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment on a study tour at Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri Province, organized by the Ministry of Environment and NGOs. WWF from 15 to 19 January 2021.
Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary, the heart of Cambodia's eastern highland landscape, is seen by officials as a potential source of ecotourism, as there are a number of rare species living in the mangrove forest. You like jungle adventures.
Although it is not yet planned to become a tourist destination, the Ministry of Environment and partner organizations are looking at the potential in this vast protected area.
At this point, Mr. Seng Teak said that when attracting tourists to visit the sanctuary to be organized according to the standard, it will create job opportunities for Cambodians living in natural resource conservation areas, it will be More sources of income for them. But this work is difficult because protected areas are areas that people are not allowed to visit or pass without permission by the law on protected areas.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, the Ministry of Environment and partner organizations are working to preserve the natural resources of the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary well before considering the establishment of a tourist attraction.
The senior official of the Ministry of Environment said that the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary is divided into four areas: core area, conservation area, sustainable use area and community area. Therefore, the core area is not allowed for people to enter. For the protected area, there is intrusion, but nonetheless, park rangers and rangers have made regular patrols to prevent crime.
(Phnom Penh): So far, the Cambodian Underwater and War Remnants Clearance Force has launched an operation to extract 5 tons of ammunition from the large amount of munitions that sank with an old ship in Veal village, Meanchey commune, Srey Santhor district, Kampong Cham province. During the war. This was confirmed by the UXO Clearance Commander of the National Center for the Management of Peacekeeping Forces, Demining and Remnants of War (NPMEC).
According to the commander, the ammunition that the special forces collected was more than 5 tons, including: 81 mm mortar, 60 mm mortar and 12.7 mm machine guns, and a total of more than 300 large cases.
Major General Mey Sophea added, "According to the plan, it will be completed in May before the rainy season, but this is not the plan of the expert team, especially for the actual situation, the expert team is difficult to conclude because the size of the ship is large and we "The total weight of the ship has not yet been estimated."
According to the team's report, the operation to clear unexploded ordnance under the water around the wreck site was 1,000 meters by 1,500 meters along the river, about 150 hectares.
National Association of Capital and Provincial Councils, Leaders Meeting to Set Work Directions for 2021
(Phnom Penh): On January 21, 2021, Mr. Pa Socheatvong, President of Phnom Penh Capital Council and President of National Association of Capital and Provincial Councils, chaired the 4th meeting of 13 Association leaders in order to review the work progress in 2020 and Discuss the work direction in 2021 to achieve the mission of the National Association as a representative and seek support for the common interests of the Capital and Provincial Councils throughout the country.
There are 13 leaders of the National Association of the second mandate (11 from the Cambodian People's Party, 1 from FUNCINPEC and 1 from the Khmer United National Party).
The meeting was held on the following agenda:
* First: Discuss the organization of the National Council meeting to summarize the work achievements in 2020 and raise the work direction in 2021 * Second: Discuss the activities not implemented in 2020 to be included in the work direction in 2021, including :
* Third: Discuss the production of membership cards of the National Rescue Association * Fourth: Check the administrative and financial work of the National Association * Fifth: Other issues.
The National Association was established as a "legal entity" to represent the Capital and Provincial Council for three purposes as defined in the statute:
* First: to enhance the status and capacity building of the Capital and Provincial Councils * Second: to protect the legitimate interests and interests of the members * Third: to build partnerships with relevant ministries and institutions to develop the grassroots, strengthen the rule of law and promote doctrine Democracy in Cambodia. Under USAID Assistance, 7 Facebook Digital Marketing Trainers Receive Certificates from Women Entrepreneurship Project
