Ta Ong Bridge, nearly 900 years old, was first repaired by APSARA Authority
(Siem Reap): Ta Ong or Ta Ong Bridge is almost 900 years old, while today it is very dilapidated, causing many parts of the ancient bridge to break apart, and in the middle of the bridge on both sides of the bridge collapsed and Other parts are also facing failure, only to be repaired for the first time by the APSARA Authority.
Ta Ong or Ta Ong Bridge, located in Chong Spean Village, Khvav Commune, Chi Kreng District, Siem Reap Province, is the longest of the 23 ancient bridges on the ancient road from Angkor to Preah Khan, Kampong Svay.
According to Mr. Puth Soth, an official of the Department of Preservation of Temples Outside Angkor Park and the head of the Ta Ong Bridge Rehabilitation Project, the APSARA Authority has started the first phase of the project to repair two main points: on the north wall of the bridge Fall and have large tears out facing the risk of falling.
In the future, the team will quote the stone registration plan, repair the stone, strengthen the fallen stone pillars, compact the soil at the base of the western bridge and prepare the dragon handrails to put them back in their original position. .
The Toong or Ta Ong Bridge is 70 meters long, 13 meters wide and has 14 ditch holes, while each ditch is 2 meters wide and 6 meters high. The bridge is made of laterite, decorated with 9-headed dragon handrails and has a Buddha meditation in the center of the sandstone dragon head.
According to Buddha Soth, it is not clear when this ancient bridge was built during the reign of any king, but it is generally known that this bridge was built during the Angkorian period, but if you look at the dragon railings, it can be seen that In the Bayon style during the reign of Jayavarman VII from 1181-1220 AD.
It should be noted that this ancient bridge, which is almost 900 years old, was celebrated in Krong Pali on January 15, 2021 in the presence of Mr. Kim Sothin, Deputy Director General of APSARA Authority and his colleagues, together with the district governor, district chief and commune chief. Village chiefs, military police and many villagers. The cost of the project to repair the ancient bridge is funded by the APSARA Authority, which will be completed in June 2021.
