(Prey Veng): The Prey Veng Provincial Gendarmerie, in cooperation with the Preah Sdach District Gendarmerie, cracked down on four drug trafficking suspects, along with 18 packages of drug nodes and other equipment and supplies in Krasang Tong village, Angkor Reach commune, Preah Sdach district. Prey Veng Province.
Lieutenant Colonel Phat Sochout, Director of the Prey Veng Provincial Gendarmerie Department of Justice, said on the afternoon of January 20, 2021 that the operation to crack down on drug trafficking and use took place in Krasang Tong village, Angkor Reach commune, Preah Sdach district on the 19th. January under the direct command of Brigadier General Chhuon Kimsan, Commander of the Provincial Gendarmerie, under the coordination of Mr. Bou Bunhang, Prosecutor of the Provincial Court.
Mr. Phat Socheat continued that before reaching the arrest of the four suspects, his forces spent several days monitoring the suspects until they identified the suspects and then used the incitement to trap the suspects and embraced them. Catch the whole team at once.
Currently, the suspect and the exhibits were detained and kept at the Provincial Gendarmerie Command to build a case to be sent to the Prey Veng Provincial Court to proceed with legal procedures.
5th Military Academy, 5th Military Region, inspected the AK 47 live ammunition training course
This training was conducted by 101 participants at the actual shooting range of the 5th Military Academy, 5th Military Zone, located in Indchit Village, Chheu Teal Commune, Banan District, Battambang Province.
The actual shooting ceremony at this time has three gestures: 1st: sleeping gesture, 2nd: Kavikat, kneeling and 3rd: Standing posture, after the shooting, the result is very good, 3 people, 17 good people and Average rating of 81 people.
Brigadier General Suon Son praised all the trainees who worked hard to get good results in real shooting training now and instructed all trainees to continue to pay more attention to learning from the military to take Knowledge gained from the school to serve its unit and homeland. Raising fish, frogs and snails helps farmers in Teuk Phos district, Kampong Chhnang province to earn money to support their families easily
(Kampong Chhnang): Due to the spread of Kovid 19 disease all over the world, some companies, factories and enterprises were closed, causing people to lose their jobs and lose income to support their families.
Some people have to move to find other jobs to earn a living. In fact, Mr. Soy Channath, a farmer living in Chiep commune, Teuk Phos district, Kampong Chhnang province, because he did not give up on the situation, he turned to raising fish, frogs, snails for sale and helped to earn a good living. Better.
In the past year, he has sold catfish three times, selling for 8,000 riel per kilogram, earning a decent amount of money to support his family. At the same time, he has also expanded his agricultural business, such as raising chickens and ducks. And grow vegetables to supply the family and sell to local farmers, most of whom do not dare to go to the market to buy fish, meat and vegetables to eat daily.
At the same time, he is raising snails and frogs in two experimental rubber ponds, in which he bred females by feeding them about 400 natural foods that can be sold for 10,000 per kilogram. Riel. As for the snails, he raises them in a rubber tent by feeding them vegetable waste and has to raise them for three months before they grow up and can be sold for 8,000 riel per kilo.
Agriculture Minister tells investors that Cambodia's agricultural sector has potential but lacks investment
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that currently the agricultural sector in Cambodia has potential, but also lacks investors to invest in this sector, both domestically and abroad.
Mr. Veng Sakhon said, "Cambodia's agriculture has potential, but Cambodia still lacks domestic and foreign investors to use the potential to the fullest to improve productivity, competitive production, processing and export to meet market demand: production standards, supply quantity, quality, safety and "Price competition."
Mr. Veng Sakhon praised Mr. Chao Inter and his team for their efforts to prepare investment projects on three main subjects:
1. Invest in medicinal plants. 2. Establish research institutes for medicinal plants, agricultural crops and organic fertilizer production. 3. Establish special economic zones to serve the agricultural sector, concentrate agricultural products, cold storage, processing and export.
He stressed that the Chinese government's investment assistance program is important to deepen the solidarity, friendship and cooperation between the two countries and peoples.
The Minister also highlighted the company's objectives:
1: Invest in the cultivation of medicinal plants, refining, processing and production.
2: Establish research institutes for medicinal plants, agricultural crops and organic fertilizer production.
3. Establish special economic zones to serve the agricultural sector, concentrate agricultural products, cold storage, processing and export.
The Minister informed that the Royal Government as well as the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries would like to welcome, support and fully cooperate to contribute to the development of medicinal plants and agriculture in the future.
Mr. Oknha Chow Enter thanked the Minister for taking the time to receive the delegation and highly appreciated and commended the cooperation of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries in its willingness to participate in the development of Cambodian medicinal plants, as well as Future agriculture.
Mr. Oknha stated that after receiving the advice from the Minister on August 6, 2020, the working group visited to study the quality of land, water sources, agro-environment, roads and transportation in the target provinces: Mondulkiri, Oddar Meanchey, Stung Treng. And Koh Kong Province to be selected as a place to establish an institute for the cultivation and research of medicinal plants and a factory for processing and exporting packaging.
Separately, Kampong Speu province chose to create a special economic zone for use in the agro-industrial sector. Currently, some sites have been selected as a priority and are awaiting the final evaluation, and legal documents are being prepared to be submitted to the Council for the Development of Cambodia (CDC) for review.
