(Kandal): Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, said that Kandal Province is one of the target provinces of the second phase of rural road improvement project under a grant from the Government of the People's Republic of China, which has received three paved roads. Total length 14 km and 350 meters.
This statement was made on the occasion of the visit of a doctor to inspect the construction site of three DBST two-story paved roads in Kandal province on Wednesday morning, May 7, 2012. 2564 is on January 20, 2021, accompanied by the Secretary of State, Undersecretary of State, Ministry Leader, Mr. Tang Mengleang, Deputy Governor of Kandal Province, as well as many other professional officials of the Ministry of Rural Development.
The project to build 3 paved roads with a total length of 14 km and 350 meters, which is currently being promoted are: The first line is 3,984 meters long, connecting National Road No. 6 at the point of the 19th bridge to Svay Rumpear village, commune. Wat Chas, Muk Kampoul District, Kandal Province. The result is about 83%.
Separately, in Kandal province, there are 3 DBST roads with a total length of 14 km and 350 meters, including the construction of drainage canals on both sides of the road with a length of 1,952 meters. 50 meters, total length 575 meters, road construction, intersection, traffic signs and safety places.
Currently, the construction of the three roads in Kandal province has reached about 60% and is scheduled to finish paving the first layer before April 2021 and complete the whole project in the second quarter of 2022.
The Minister thanked the people as well as the Government of the People's Republic of China for their continued assistance to Cambodia in the field of rural infrastructure and transportation, and reminded the technical staff on site to adhere to the technical standards for use. Long lasting.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra: The ranger of the Ministry of Environment continues the technical training to strengthen the capacity to increase the efficiency of protection and conservation of natural resources.
(Mondulkiri): "Although park rangers have a lot of experience in patrolling, cracking down on crime, protecting and conserving natural resources, all rangers must continue to train in new techniques to increase their capacity and gain new lessons and experiences." In addition, in order to increase the efficiency of its role as a forest guard.
This was stated by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment in front of the park rangers working in Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary and Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri Province on January 17, 2021 in Ceremony of awarding the Certificate of Environmental Peace to the outstanding rangers of the two sanctuaries.
The award ceremony was organized as part of a press conference co-organized by the Ministry of Environment and WWF with nearly 40 journalists from more than 20 institutions participating.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that prior to his inauguration, the rangers of the Ministry of Environment, who were selected, received professional training on patrol methods and other standards, including the law on the use of firearms. On protected areas and their professional ethics and other relevant approaches.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra emphasized that continuing the capacity building training is very important because the work of the rangers of the rangers is very risky, some of the perpetrators of natural resources use both real and processed weapons, including potential accidents. With all kinds of traps set by criminals in the forest to catch wild animals illegally.
At Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary and Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary, with the technical support of WWF, rangers have been trained in new techniques, as well as raises for rangers.
Six outstanding park rangers received certificates of commendation from the Ministry of Environment, including three rangers working at Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary and three others actively working in Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri Province. Park rangers, who are considered by the Ministry of Environment to be forest rangers and at the forefront of conserving natural resources.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, who handed over the Certificate of Environmental Peace directly to the rangers at the Trapeang Thmear Wildlife Sanctuary at the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary, said that the Certificate of Environmental Peace is a testament to the courageous dedication of the body. Natural resource protection in Cambodia.
At the same time, senior officials of the Ministry of Environment also encouraged park rangers to continue to work responsibly and continue to increase their professional capacity. He also thanked the families of the rangers, especially the parents, spouses / children of the rangers for allowing their children or spouses to participate in the conservation of natural resources, which is a very challenging task, especially with the people. Offense.
In addition to receiving commendation certificates from the Ministry of Environment, all rangers at Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary and Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary also received commendation letters from WWF, a partner of the Ministry of Environment.
Mr. Ban Seng Teak stated that WWF would also like to present a letter of commendation for the work of all rangers from both sanctuaries to provide support and encouragement to our front line officials. Strong will, commitment and high dedication to protect natural resources and wildlife, which is a living national heritage for future generations.
Former hunter-gatherer turns into park ranger, "Don't kill wildlife for business like farming"
(Mondulkiri): Former Khmer Rouge soldier and hunter until he became an outstanding park ranger in Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary, Mondulkiri Province, Mr. Min Thoeun sent a message: "Stop, stop! Do not take the killing of wild animals for business as farming.
Mr. Min Thoeun made the remarks in an interview with reporters during a five-day visit to the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary in the eastern highland landscape area of Mondulkiri province last weekend. .
After working as a park ranger for almost 10 years at Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary, Mr. Min Thoeun became a model official who received a letter of commendation from the Ministry of Environment for dedicating his time, day and night, and his life to protect natural resources. .
It took more than 40 years to complete the magnificent Royal Palace. By 1682, King Louis XIV had moved to the new palace before it was completely rebuilt and moved to a new location. After the reign of Louis XIV, the Palace of Versailles continued to be the residence of later French emperors, who further adorned the palace.
Rooms in the National Museum of Versailles have been transformed into a gallery of new paintings and sculptures of great personalities and paintings related to major events in French history. At the beginning of the 20th century, the number of these paintings and sculptures increased exponentially, and the government restored parts damaged during the revolution to the original palaces.
