Farmers in Prasat Balang district, Kampong Thom province, happy after earning income from raising chickens as a family
(Kampong Thom): It is really amazing for a farmer family living in Boss Sramoch village, Chey commune, Prasat Balong district, Kampong Thom province, after trying to overcome all obstacles to succeed in raising chickens as a family and Can earn a high income to support the family without having to migrate or work to earn a monthly salary.
He said that at first he only raised chickens for eggs to supply the breeding team, but because he was divided into egg breeders, hatcheries and other broilers, then he raised chickens to produce eggs. Community members, he has no buyers for incubators, so he decided to buy incubators for himself by incubating both eggs and meat.
As a chicken farmer, Mr. Ton Ton called on all farmers to raise more chickens to improve their livelihood, especially to reduce the migration to another district is to make the country. We also have food security and reduce imports.
Mr. Pen Vanrith, Director of Kampong Thom Provincial Department of Agriculture, praised Mr. Ton Ton's family for taking into account the well-being of the people and promoting more chicken raising to supply the local market, reducing imports, following the advice of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
Mr. Pen Vanrith said that he will try to facilitate the technical increase production to reduce the import of animals from external sources and increase employment for people in the country. ADB: Industry 4.0 May Lead to Skills and Job Transformation in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia should consider developing an industrial transformation map for key sectors to support the transition to the Fourth Industrial Revolution (4IR) with sufficient investment to strengthen skills for new and changing jobs. . This is according to a new study by the Asian Development Bank (ADB).
The findings are one of six recommendations from the ADB study on Industry 4.0 Benefits through Skills Development in Cambodia. It is part of a study focusing on four ASEAN countries, including Cambodia, Indonesia, the Philippines and Vietnam.
This study looks at the garment and tourism industry in Cambodia, as both sectors are important for job growth, international competitiveness, and 4IR. It found that 4IR technology would eliminate jobs in the garment and tourism industries, but this loss would be offset by increased demand brought about by higher productivity, which could create 39% job growth for the garment industry and 2%. For tourism.
Although the results of job creation are generally positive in both sectors, the study warns that there is no guarantee that workers whose jobs have been changed will be able to easily find newly created jobs without adequate and timely investment. Time to develop skills. Job changes are also likely to affect about 81% of women in Cambodia's garment industry.
The study calls for new approaches to strengthening the environment and social protection for new workers, at-risk workers with changes in employment and workers in need of additional skills.
This study encourages Cambodia to develop a Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) program with a focus on 4IR accreditation in the garment and tourism industries and a flexible certification program that recognizes competency. Outside the traditional education system.
The study also calls for implementing incentive projects for companies to train their employees about 4IR, which can be assisted by the Cambodian Skills Development Fund. In the tourism sector, policymakers are encouraged to encourage the development of smartphone applications to build readiness for 4IR, ensure communication, social and communication skills, and foster greater collaboration between training institutions and employers.
Employers surveyed in garment manufacturing (90%) and tourism (79%) reported that graduates hired last year were not well prepared for their first job position. This is contrary to the perception of training institutions that show that almost 60% of graduates are ready for work.
While the Kovid 19 pandemic is accelerating its digital transformation, studies have found that companies that embark on 4IR technology are more likely to recover from the crisis caused by the pandemic and be resilient in the future. . Cambodian Foreign Minister to Attend ASEAN Foreign Ministers' Meeting on January 21 via Video
