Ministry of Environment supports WWF to bring live pepper planting techniques to farmers to reduce the use of natural wood
(Mondulkiri): On behalf of the Minister of Environment, Mr. Neth Pheaktra expressed his support for WWF, which brought pepper planting techniques using live tree trunks to about 70 families in Putang village, Pu Prey commune. Pichreada district, Mondulkiri province, as a contribution to reducing the use of natural wood.
Such support from Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment was made on the occasion of leading a group of national and international journalists on a study tour of Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri Province, organized by the Ministry of Environment and NGOs. WWF from 15 to 19 January 2021.
Mr. Pheaktra said that the Ministry of Environment is pushing for a solution to the reduction of natural wood, and that the solution that WWF allows people to plant cilantro as a pepper plant is a new option to reduce the use of natural wood.
The Secretary of State stressed that "reducing the pressure on natural timber is one solution, and another point that our Ministry of Environment is pushing and supporting is to promote plantations that can be harvested and used." "Without removing natural wood."
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, the current global trend is to stop using natural wood and people are beginning to be educated that the use of natural wood is a pressure on natural wood, so investing in plantations is necessary to Reduce the pressure on natural wood, and today in Cambodia, some companies have started to invest in plantations such as oil palm, acacia, which can be used for five years.
A senior official of the Ministry of Environment also stated: "In a year, Cambodia needs to use more than 2 million cubic meters of wood, furniture and construction wood, so the use of live pepper is part of Reducing the pressure of natural wood.
In this regard, Net Pheaktra thanked WWF for bringing the new technology of pepper cultivation using live tunnels and wanted to expand to some provinces, starting from Mondulkiri to Tbong Khmum, Kratie, Ratanakkiri. Kampot and Kep.
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF, said, “Pepper plantations are part of the deforestation in the study. "Seven hectares of forest are gone from the rainforest , so we need to find other options to make the same pepper plantation, we can get a similar yield without destroying the rainforest to make pepper."
Mr. Seng Teak said that WWF programs help communities help families to understand new technologies, move forward and help preserve our forests. WWF is currently implementing this pepper program for 70 families by 2020. The project will expand to 60 more families in 2021 for families who are interested in this new technology to help with forest conservation.
