Samdech Techo Hun Sen's memorandum of understanding was made on the application letter of Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, made on January 13, 2021.
With this description of the encroachment on state land as private property, Mr. Veng Sakhon proposed to Samdech Techo Hun Sen four points to prevent and prevent the spread of recurring encroachment on state land. As well as protecting the legitimate interests of investors. These four proposals include:
1. The provincial administration must issue a letter of confirmation of any documents issued by the village, commune, district or provincial authorities that are not in accordance with the previous procedures and must issue an order to confiscate these lands as state property.
3. The provincial administration must prepare an action plan to launch a large-scale campaign to clear all forms of illegal encroachment and encroachment on state land, with the joint support of national forces.
4: The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries shall examine the possibility of requesting regulation or suing the court for annulment of immovable property certificates that have not completed the full procedures in force.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also agreed to these four proposals. According to sources, after the request, the relevant ministries and provincial authorities held a meeting to implement all these procedures.
Please note that the encroachment on state land as personal property is not new for Mondulkiri province. In 2020, there was also a review of land encroachment cases in Oreang and Keo Seima districts. At that time, the people involved in issuing the land purchase and sale documents were fined, and the governor of Koh Nhek district, Mr. Sin Vanvuth, was also removed from office.
