Mr. Neth Pheaktra confirmed that in Mondulkiri province, nearly 10,000 traps will be removed from protected areas by 2020. He said park rangers not only patrolled to prevent crime, destruction of natural resources, deforestation, but also rangers searched for traps and removed them, as well as destroyed traps to save wildlife.
In addition to traps, which are hidden killers that have killed countless wildlife, Neth Pheaktra said the overall factors contributing to the decline in wildlife include habitat loss, which is a biological problem. Diversification sometimes leads to conflicts between animals and humans. In addition, hunting is also a point that threatens the loss of wildlife.
He pointed out that the increase in recycled guns is also a real threat to wildlife. In 2020, rangers confiscated more than 700 rifles from hunters in protected areas across the country. This is also the most dangerous weapon.
In connection with the deforestation, Mr. Neth Pheaktra also mentioned that chasing dogs, which people live in areas where there are wild animals, and some people have raised herds of chasing dogs to go out hunting. Contributing factors reduce the number of wildlife.
According to the spokesman, in order to stop hunting, stop trapping animals, people should not eat meat, do not trade in deer, and do not believe in the belief that they eat meat, blood or take wild bones for medicine. Tonic or health aid, but it may put you at risk because of the possibility of various viruses.
Despite reports of this alarming decline, there is still hope for rescuing these wildlife from extinction. But without urgent and innovative conservation measures to curb these major threats, biodiversity will continue to decline gradually until it is completely extinct.
Mr. Seng Teak said that it is not too late to change the situation, but it requires us to take immediate joint conservation action now, with the participation of all stakeholders.
"The findings of the scientists in this report highlight the need for urgent and innovative solutions to address this decline while seeking to manage the use and allocation of natural resources," he said. "Better than this."
The Ministry of Environment and WWF have been working closely with local communities and partners to develop stronger conservation measures to reduce poaching and strengthen law enforcement. The Ministry of Environment and WWF are studying the possibility of implementing a program to restore the toenails, which is an urgent need to stop the declining trend of these species and take action to address the root causes of the illegal wildlife trade.
The handover ceremony was attended by representatives of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, representatives of the Council of Ministers, officials of the Chinese Embassy in Cambodia and leaders of the Mine Authority.
