"Bear Bear of Hope" project designed to help underprivileged communities and conserve wildlife
(Phnom Penh): Two cute teddy bear dolls made by women from poor communities are now on sale at NagaWorld. This is part of NagaWorld's "Teddy Bear of Hope" initiative.
Proceeds from the sale of these adorable teddy bears will be donated to artisans and widows economically affected by Kovid-19 and to support the conservation of wildlife in Cambodia.
The women are trained to make souvenirs made from the highest quality, most beautiful and attractive yarn. This craft offers options and job opportunities so they can earn an income for their family.
Widows in poor communities with financial problems caused by Kovid-19 will also benefit from the sale.
Located in the Phnom Tamao Wildlife Rescue Center in Takeo province, the shelter provides daily shelter, food and care for about 120 bears rescued from the illegal trade.
NagaWorld Managing Director Pern Chen said, "Through the teddy bear program of hope, NagaWorld aims to improve the lives of people who rely on handicrafts in the country."
With the gestures and generosity of the guests who help buy the teddy bear, it will help many families who are in need, especially in this difficult time of Kovid-19 disease.
It is also a great way to save the lives of our precious wildlife, with the proceeds from the sale of the dolls going to be used to protect and conserve Cambodian bears in their natural habitats. ”
According to Guatemalan authorities, between 7,000 and 8,000 refugees, including children, have been arriving in Guatemala since last Friday. Most of them are refugees from Honduras fleeing the violence, poverty and economic crisis caused by COVID-19.
