Every year, 10,000 patients seek counseling on mental health issues at the Cambodian Intercultural Psychosocial Organization
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Chhim Sotheara, Executive Director of the Cambodian Intercultural Psychosocial Organization (TPO), an organization focusing on mental health issues in Cambodia, said that the number of patients coming for treatment and consultation on mental health problems is increasing. And reach 10,000 people a year.
This statement was made by Dr. Chhim Sotheara during the "Mental Health Awareness Project" in collaboration with Save the Children Foundation, in collaboration with Ms. Chea Serey, Founder of Save the Children, on 16 January. This 2021.
The doctor added that seeing these issues, the TPO has developed a "mental health awareness project" that will encourage patients to better understand and find help in a timely manner. He added that the most common age for mental health problems is between 20 and 50 years old, but recently a large number of older people have come to consult about mental illness.
During the outbreak of Kovid 19, the number of people with mental health problems also increased, he said.
He said that in collaboration with the Save the Children Foundation on the "Mental Health Awareness Project" for a three-month trial, but in the past, the Save the Children Fund has been helping the TPO for about three years on the chain release project. Focuses on many serious mental illnesses that lack access to treatment and are far from incurable.
Ms. Chea Serey said that at present, while Cambodia is facing the problem of Kovid 19, it is seen that people are under a lot of emotional pressure, with some losing their job, income or being away from their family as a psychological problem. This psychological awareness will find timely and helpful help.
