General knowledge: Singapore does not have its own fresh water source, what can be done to get water?
(Singapore): There are many island nations that do not have their own freshwater resources, and Singapore has the same fate as those countries, but do you know what the government of this small country is doing to get Fresh water for people to drink and use daily.
Of the countries without their own freshwater resources, Singapore seems to be the luckiest because it is rich and close to rich freshwater resources such as Malaysia. More than 30 years ago, Singapore imported only fresh water from Malaysia through a sea pipeline from Johor, Malaysia. Imported fresh water is up to 250 million barrels per day.
The water used in the house will be filtered by high technology by separating the dirty molecules and filtered, then sprayed with ultraviolet light to kill germs, which is the final stage. Clean water for re-use, and seawater will be filtered to remove salt and sent to produce clean water, then distributed at home.
The water used in Singapore today comes from four sources: rainwater, freshwater imported from Malaysia, filtered water and seawater that filter salt.
