(Mondulkiri): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, on January 15, 2021, led a group of national and international journalists from 20 institutions to visit Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary for 5 days. Of the eastern highland landscape forest area in Mondulkiri province.
The visit starts on the 15th and ends on the 19th of January 2021, jointly organized by the Ministry of Environment and WWF, with the participation of the leaders and officials of the Ministry and the Department of Environment, Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF In all, more than 50 key WWF staff members.
The press visit is designed to provide an opportunity for Cambodian journalists and participants to learn about the importance and potential of the Srepok Wildlife Sanctuary, which is at the heart of the Eastern Highlands landscape for the conservation of natural resources and biodiversity. Diversity and for the restoration of important wildlife species of global value to contribute to the development of local communities and the national economy.
The senior official of the Ministry of Environment added: "This information and knowledge will contribute to fostering love for the environment, natural resources and biodiversity among the growing public and inspire people to participate in the care and protection. "Precious natural resources for the nation of Cambodia, as well as for the world as a whole."
For more than two decades, WWF has been working closely with the Royal Government of Cambodia in the field of natural resource management and protection. In close cooperation with the Ministry of Environment and other partners, WWF is supporting the management and conservation of rich biodiversity resources in the Sre Pok and Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary on a total area of approximately 6,000 square kilometers is located in Mondulkiri province.
Seng Teak, Director of WWF, said: “This visit will provide an opportunity for journalists to gain access to new information and knowledge that can broaden the public media vision for their contribution to the promotion of unparalleled importance and value. Two of these landscapes to the Cambodian people.
"Through this visit, the good partnership between WWF and the Ministry of Environment is further strengthened to continue the close cooperation in the cause of managing, restoring and protecting natural resources," he said. "And many species of wildlife that are valuable for global conservation, as well as for the continued development of the livelihoods of local communities in the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary and the Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary."
