Officials temporarily closed two soybean bubble handicrafts in Khsach Kandal district after this handicraft produced chemicals
(Kandal): Officers of the Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud Branch (Kandal Province) cooperated with officials of the Department of Commerce and local authorities with the approval of the Kandal Provincial Prosecutor. On the morning of January 14, 2021, two soybean bubble handicrafts were temporarily closed in Koh Touch village and Kbal Koh village, Koh Oknha Tey commune, Khsach Kandal district, Kandal province, which produced soybean foam with boracic chemicals.
Mr. Touch Chao Koy, Branch Manager of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud (Kandal) Branch of Kandal Province, said that as a result of the preliminary test of the soybean foam handicraft in Kbal Koh village, it contained boracic chemicals and The sodium hydroxide (powder) team also confiscated the finished product, stored it, and made a contract to temporarily stop the craft and to make modifications.
Another bean bubble handicraft in Koh Touch village also contains chemicals, but since it was the first day of production, officials also contracted to stop adding chemicals.
Mr. Toch Chao Koy said that in the case of the above two handicrafts, if there is a second time, legal procedures will be implemented.
(Svay Rieng): Born in the Odong period, Wat Svay Chrum was first built in 1770 with the devotion of Buddhists and people both at home and abroad.
Currently, Svay Chrum pagoda is located in Boeung Veng village, Svay Chrum commune, Svay Chrum district, Svay Rieng province, north of National Road 1, 10 km from the provincial town.
According to 88-year-old Acha Nam Anson, Svay Chrum pagoda is one of the oldest pagodas in Svay Rieng province. Every day, people from far and near come to pray for their wishes, including asking for children, selling land, taking oaths, etc., and some of those prayers are answered. Desire, therefore, makes a large number of people have faith in the power of Wat Svay Chrum.
Mr. Tiv Sophea stated that the reason why it is called Wat Svay Chrum is because this pagoda is rich in mango trees that many locals have planted, so it is called Wat Svay Chrum until today.
The reason why Buddhists believe in the sacred power of the great Buddha in this pagoda is because it is said that one day the Buddhists saw the Buddha's head floating in the water on the river nearby, so they collected and built it. The pagoda is considered a shrine to this day.
He added that from 1975 to 1979, some of them were destroyed by the Khmer Rouge Pol Pot, leaving only the basement of a school, a hut, and two stone huts, two of which were Khmer Rouge workplaces.
